/* Muraqba Academy — Google Ads course data (extracted from course file, content unchanged) */
const GDATA = {"meta":{"course":"google","title":"Google Ads","tagline":"From zero to hero","modules":11},"modules":[{"id":"foundations","n":1,"title":"Foundations","sub":"What Google Ads actually is, and how the machine underneath it works.","intro":"Google Ads is an auction that runs every time someone searches or watches something. This module gives you the map: where ads show, how an account is organised, how a winner is chosen, and why 2026 rewards good data over clever keyword lists.","sections":[{"id":"where","h":"Where Google ads show up","html":"<p>Your ads can appear on <strong>Search</strong> results, in <strong>Shopping</strong> listings, on <strong>YouTube</strong>, across partner sites and apps in the <strong>Display Network</strong>, and on Gmail, Discover and Maps through <strong>Performance Max</strong> and <strong>Demand Gen</strong> campaigns. Each surface meets people at a different moment.</p><p>Search shows ads to people who typed a question. YouTube and Discover show them to people who are browsing. That difference decides which campaign type you choose later.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":"f-place"},{"id":"structure","h":"The structure you will live in","html":"<p>Work flows down five levels. A <strong>Manager account</strong> (MCC) groups accounts. An <strong>account</strong> holds billing, currency and time zone. A <strong>campaign</strong> sets the type, budget, bid strategy and locations. An <strong>ad group</strong> holds one theme of keywords. <strong>Ads and assets</strong> are what people see.</p><p>Most mistakes come from putting a setting at the wrong level. Budget and goals live on the campaign; messaging lives in the ad group and assets.</p>","callout":null,"figure":"structure","widget":null},{"id":"auction","h":"How the auction decides a winner","html":"<p>Every eligible ad gets an <strong>Ad Rank</strong>, built from your bid, <strong>expected CTR</strong>, ad relevance, <strong>landing page experience</strong>, the impact of assets and the context of the search. Highest rank wins the best position.</p><p>You do not pay your max bid. You pay about what you need to stay ahead of the next ad. That is why better quality lowers cost: it lets a smaller bid win.</p>","callout":{"k":"warn","t":"The simulator is a simplification. Google does not publish the exact formula, so use it to build intuition, not to predict real costs."},"figure":"adrank","widget":"f-auction"},{"id":"intent","h":"Intent vs interruption","html":"<p><strong>Search</strong> captures demand that already exists: the person is asking for what you sell. It converts well but is capped by how many people search. <strong>YouTube</strong> and <strong>Demand Gen</strong> create interest in people who were not looking yet.</p><p>Beginners should usually start with Search, because the signal is clear and the results are easy to read. Add interruption formats when search volume runs out.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":null},{"id":"ai","h":"The 2026 shift: AI-first matching","html":"<p>Google now predicts intent from many signals instead of matching your words literally. <strong>Broad match</strong> with <strong>Smart Bidding</strong>, <strong>AI Max for Search</strong> and Performance Max all work this way, so tight manual keyword control is weaker than it used to be.</p><p>What you still control: conversion tracking, the quality of your assets, your landing pages, your negative keywords and your budget. Feed the system good data and clear exclusions, and review the <strong>search terms</strong> report often.</p>","callout":{"k":"info","t":"Feature names and settings change often. Check the current Google Ads Help Centre before relying on a specific option."},"figure":null,"widget":"f-ai"}],"takeaways":["Search captures existing demand; YouTube and Demand Gen create it.","Accounts nest from Manager account down to ads and assets, and each level controls different things.","Ad Rank mixes bid and quality, and you pay just enough to beat the next ad.","In the AI-first era your levers are data, assets, exclusions and budget.","The search terms report shows what people actually typed."],"action":"Search for something you would buy this week. Screenshot the results page and label every ad: which parts are headline, URL, description and assets?","quiz":[{"q":"What do you pay when you win an auction?","opts":["Your max bid","About what you need to beat the next ad","A fixed price per click"],"correct":1,"explain":"You pay the minimum needed to hold your position, which can be well below your max bid."},{"q":"Which surface mostly captures existing demand?","opts":["Search","YouTube","Display"],"correct":0,"explain":"Search shows ads to people already asking for something."},{"q":"In the AI-first era, which is a lever you still control?","opts":["Exactly which queries match","Conversion tracking quality","Competitors' bids"],"correct":1,"explain":"Good conversion data is what Smart Bidding learns from."}],"related":[{"t":"Visual Library","d":"See the diagrams in one place.","to":"visuals"},{"t":"Glossary: Ad Rank","d":"Definition and context.","to":"glossary/Ad Rank"},{"t":"Glossary: Smart Bidding","d":"How automated bidding works.","to":"glossary/Smart Bidding"}]},{"id":"setup","n":2,"title":"Account Setup & Infrastructure","sub":"The plumbing that has to be right before you spend a cent.","intro":"Nothing in the modules ahead works if the plumbing is wrong. A properly structured account, a tag that fires reliably, and conversions that mean what you think they mean are the difference between Smart Bidding learning the right lesson and learning nothing at all. This module is about getting that foundation right once, before a live budget is depending on it.","sections":[{"id":"mcc","h":"Manager account vs standard account","html":"<p>A <strong>Manager account (MCC)</strong> is not itself an advertising account — it holds no budget and runs no ads. It is a login that gives you access to one or more standard Ads accounts, with combined billing visibility, shared reporting, and the ability to move between accounts without separate passwords. If you plan to manage more than one account, ever, create the MCC first and link accounts into it rather than owning them directly.</p><p>A standard account is where the real work happens: campaigns, budgets, billing details, currency and time zone. Freelancers sometimes skip the MCC when they only have one client, then regret it the moment a second client arrives and the first account was never set up to be linked cleanly.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":"s-portfolio"},{"id":"create","h":"Creating the account properly","html":"<p>When Google first prompts you through account creation, it defaults to a simplified Smart setup aimed at people who want a campaign running in minutes (the exact flow changes, so check the current Google Ads Help Centre). Skip it. That mode trades away match-type control, most reporting detail, and proper conversion tracking — all recoverable later, but the detour costs time you don't need to spend.</p><p>Two settings become permanent the moment you confirm them: <strong>currency</strong> and <strong>time zone</strong>. There is no in-product way to change either afterward; the only fix is creating a new account and starting over. Set both deliberately, matching the currency you will actually be billed in and the time zone your reports and scheduling should run on, before anything else.</p><p>Billing itself is separate from campaign budgets — you are setting a payment method and a billing country, not a spend cap. Confirm who is named on the billing profile before launch, since changing it later can involve a support process.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":null},{"id":"tag","h":"The Google tag, Google Tag Manager and conversion actions","html":"<p>The <strong>Google tag</strong> is a single piece of website code that can send events to Google Ads, GA4 and other Google products at once. Most accounts either install it directly on every page or manage it through <strong>Google Tag Manager</strong>, a free container that lets you add, edit and remove tags without a developer touching site code for every change.</p><p>A <strong>conversion action</strong> is one trackable thing you have told Google Ads to count — a purchase, a form submission, a phone call — each with its own counting rule and, where relevant, a <strong>conversion value</strong>. Mark the actions that represent real business value as <strong>Primary conversion</strong> (often just one or two); the rest should be Secondary, visible in reporting but excluded from what automated bidding optimizes toward. Mixing the two is one of the most common reasons Smart Bidding ends up chasing the wrong goal.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":"s-data"},{"id":"consent","h":"Enhanced conversions and Consent Mode v2","html":"<p><strong>Enhanced conversions</strong> sends hashed first-party data — a hashed email address, a hashed phone number — alongside a conversion event, letting Google match it back to an ad click even when third-party cookies are unavailable. The data is hashed in the browser before it ever leaves the visitor's device; you are improving match quality, not handing over raw personal data.</p><p><strong>Consent Mode</strong> adjusts what your tags send based on the cookie consent a visitor actually gives, and can model that gap statistically rather than losing the data outright. For traffic from the EEA and UK in particular, running without it configured correctly means under-reported conversions and bid strategies learning from an incomplete picture — a measurement problem, not just a compliance checkbox.</p>","callout":{"k":"warn","t":"Consent and privacy requirements vary by region and change over time. Confirm your current obligations and the exact Consent Mode setup against the current Google Ads Help Centre, not against this course."},"figure":null,"widget":null},{"id":"linking","h":"Linking GA4, Merchant Center and YouTube","html":"<p>Linking <strong>GA4</strong> lets you import GA4-defined conversions and audiences into Google Ads instead of rebuilding tracking twice, and is usually the fastest way to get ecommerce-grade reporting without a developer project. Linking <strong>Merchant Center</strong> is not optional for Shopping campaigns or ecommerce Performance Max — it is the product feed they run from, and without it those campaign types have nothing to sell.</p><p>Linking a YouTube channel lets Demand Gen and Video campaigns use your existing content, and gives you reporting on how ads perform alongside it. None of these links cost anything to set up, and all of them should happen in the first session, before your first campaign launches, so the data is there from day one instead of backfilled later.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":null},{"id":"gclid","h":"Auto-tagging and gclid","html":"<p><strong>Auto-tagging</strong> is an account setting, on by default, that appends a <strong>gclid</strong> — a unique click identifier — to the URL of your landing page whenever someone clicks an ad. That identifier is how a conversion recorded minutes or days later gets credited back to the specific click that caused it.</p><p>If a site redirects through multiple domains, strips URL parameters, or uses a page builder that rewrites query strings, the gclid can be lost in transit and conversions quietly stop being attributed — one of the most common 'my tracking just broke' causes, and one of the first things worth checking when numbers look wrong for no obvious reason.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":null},{"id":"methods","h":"Tracking methods, side by side","html":"<p>There is no single correct tracking method; most working accounts combine two or three of the following. Treat this as a starting map, not a ranking.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":null},{"id":"basics","h":"Conversions you will actually use, and the basics people forget","html":"<p>Across most accounts, the conversion actions that matter boil down to a short list: purchase (with value, for ecommerce), lead form submit, phone call, add to cart and begin checkout (useful as secondary signals and for remarketing), and sign-up (a good proxy when the real sale happens later, offline). Pick the few that represent real business value and mark those as Primary; resist the urge to make everything primary.</p><p>Four basics get skipped constantly, and each one blocks something downstream: verifying your website, having a visible and working privacy policy page, landing pages that actually match the ad's promise, and business information — address, phone, hours — kept current wherever it feeds a Business Profile or call assets.</p>","callout":{"k":"info","t":"None of this is exciting work, which is exactly why it gets skipped. Do it before the first dollar of real budget goes live, not after a week of confusing reports."},"figure":null,"widget":"s-events"}],"takeaways":["A Manager account holds no budget; it's your access layer across one or more real accounts.","Currency and time zone are permanent the moment you confirm them.","Mark only the actions that represent real business value as Primary conversions.","Enhanced conversions and Consent Mode work together to keep measurement accurate under privacy restrictions.","A lost gclid is one of the most common silent tracking failures.","Link GA4, Merchant Center and YouTube on day one, not after launch."],"action":"Open or create your Ads account and check five things in one sitting: currency and time zone, which conversion actions are Primary, whether auto-tagging is on, whether GA4 is linked, and whether your privacy policy page actually loads.","quiz":[{"q":"Which of these can you change after confirming it during account creation?","opts":["Currency","Time zone","Your billing payment method"],"correct":2,"explain":"Currency and time zone are permanent; the payment method on file can be updated later."},{"q":"What does Enhanced conversions send to Google?","opts":["Hashed first-party data, like a hashed email","Your visitor's raw browsing history","Nothing, once Consent Mode is removed"],"correct":0,"explain":"The data is hashed in the browser before it ever leaves the visitor's device."},{"q":"A conversion stops being attributed after a site redirect. What's the most likely cause?","opts":["The gclid parameter was lost in the redirect","The ad was disapproved","The Manager account expired"],"correct":0,"explain":"Redirects, stripped parameters and some page builders can drop the gclid in transit."},{"q":"What should you mark as a Primary conversion?","opts":["Every event you track, for maximum data","Only the actions that represent real business value","Only events with no monetary value"],"correct":1,"explain":"Mixing minor signals into Primary gives automated bidding the wrong goal to chase."}],"related":[{"t":"Foundations","d":"Revisit how the auction and account structure fit together.","to":"m/foundations"},{"t":"Glossary: Enhanced conversions","d":"Definition and context.","to":"glossary/Enhanced conversions"},{"t":"Glossary: gclid","d":"What it is and why it matters.","to":"glossary/gclid"}]},{"id":"audiences","n":3,"title":"Audiences & Targeting","sub":"Keywords tell Google what people want. Audiences tell it who they are.","intro":"Keywords tell Google what someone is searching for. Audiences tell it who that person is, and what they've already done. Used well, the two work together; used carelessly, they compete with each other for the same clicks.","sections":[{"id":"segs","h":"Audience segment types","html":"<p>An <strong>audience segment</strong> is a defined group of people Google Ads can target or observe. Your-data segments come from things that have actually happened — website visitors, app users, customer lists, YouTube viewers — and are usually the highest-intent audiences available, because they're built from real behaviour rather than a prediction.</p><p>Google-built segments are predictions at scale: Affinity groups people by long-term interests and lifestyle, in-market groups people Google believes are actively comparing options right now, life events catches people around a major change such as moving or graduating, and detailed demographics groups people by a shared life fact beyond basic age and gender. Custom segments sit apart from all of these — you build them yourself from keywords, URLs or apps that describe the kind of person you're trying to reach.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":"a-types"},{"id":"toso","h":"Targeting vs Observation settings","html":"<p>The same audience segment can be applied two different ways, and the difference changes what a campaign does. <strong>Targeting</strong> restricts delivery to only that audience — your ad simply will not show to anyone outside it. <strong>Observation</strong> does the opposite: delivery stays unrestricted, and the segment is layered on top purely for reporting and bid adjustments, so you can see how that group performs without excluding anyone else.</p><p>On Search campaigns, most accounts should default to Observation. Keywords are already doing the targeting; stacking a restrictive audience on top of them usually just shrinks reach without adding precision. Targeting mode earns its place on Display and Video, where no keyword is doing that job and the audience segment is the only thing deciding who sees the ad at all.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":null},{"id":"signals","h":"Audience signals in Performance Max and Demand Gen","html":"<p>Performance Max and Demand Gen use audience segments differently again: as a signal, not a boundary. You tell the system 'start by looking for people like this,' and the AI uses that as a starting point before expanding based on what is actually converting. Unlike Targeting mode, a strong Performance Max signal will not stop the system from showing ads outside it once it finds conversions there.</p><p>This is a genuine mindset shift from keyword-era targeting, and it trips people up constantly: a weak or absent signal does not mean no targeting — it means the system is guessing with less to go on. Feed it your best available data — past purchasers, your customer list, your highest-intent custom segment — and treat the result as a head start, not a leash.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":null},{"id":"expansion","h":"Optimized targeting and audience expansion","html":"<p><strong>Optimized targeting</strong> is a feature, available in some campaign types, that can show your ads to people beyond the audience segments you selected, whenever the system predicts they're likely to convert. It's Google's version of 'trust the data more than the list you built' — reasonable once a campaign has real conversion history, riskier to lean on before it does.</p><p>The trade-off is visibility: the further a campaign leans on expansion, the less any single audience segment explains who you actually reached. That's a fair exchange once conversions are flowing and Smart Bidding has something to learn from. Early in a campaign's life, before it has that data, it's worth keeping expansion modest and watching the search terms or placement reports closely instead.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":null},{"id":"remarketing","h":"Remarketing and Customer Match","html":"<p><strong>Remarketing</strong> shows ads to people who have already interacted with your website, app or YouTube content — by a wide margin the highest-intent audience available to most advertisers, because they aren't strangers to the brand. <strong>Customer Match</strong> extends the same idea to data you already own: upload your customer list, as hashed identifiers, and Google Ads builds a segment from whichever of those people it can match to a signed-in Google account.</p><p>Customer Match is as useful for exclusion as for targeting — uploading existing customers so a prospecting campaign stops spending budget trying to acquire people you already have. Both remarketing and Customer Match sit on real personal data, so list-size minimums, consent requirements and acceptable-use rules apply; treat the privacy rules as a hard boundary, not a formality to work around.</p>","callout":{"k":"warn","t":"Customer Match and remarketing both depend on collecting and uploading real customer data lawfully. Confirm current consent, minimum list size and acceptable-use requirements against the Google Ads Help Centre before uploading any list."},"figure":null,"widget":"a-retarget"},{"id":"geo","h":"Location, language and exclusions","html":"<p>Location targeting has two distinct modes that are easy to confuse: targeting people whose presence is in your chosen location right now, versus people with interest in that location even if they're physically elsewhere. A local business usually wants presence; a destination or a nationwide ecommerce brand may genuinely want interest too. Getting this backwards either wastes budget on people researching a place they'll never visit, or silently excludes the locals you actually wanted.</p><p>Language targeting matches the language of a person's Google settings and search behaviour, not the language of your ad copy — set it to match where your actual customers are, and exclude locations you cannot legally serve, cannot fulfil to, or have deliberately decided not to pursue, rather than leaving the net as wide as possible by default.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":null},{"id":"overlap","h":"Campaign overlap and cannibalization","html":"<p>The most common structural mistake past the beginner stage is several campaigns quietly competing for the same auction. A branded Search campaign, a non-branded Search campaign and a Performance Max campaign can all become eligible for the same query, and when they do, Google picks one winner per auction — the others simply don't show, and your reporting makes it look like weak campaigns rather than overlapping ones.</p><p>Brand and non-brand should almost always be split into separate campaigns, both so you can see true non-brand performance and so cheap, high-converting brand clicks don't flatter blended numbers. Performance Max and Search can run together safely, but check the PMax channel-level reporting — not just top-line results — periodically, to see whether it's winning auctions your Search campaign would have won anyway at a lower cost.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":null}],"takeaways":["Your-data audiences are built from real behaviour and are usually your highest-intent segments.","Targeting restricts who sees your ad; Observation only measures and adjusts bids.","In Performance Max and Demand Gen, an audience segment is a signal the AI starts from, not a hard limit.","Customer Match and remarketing both run on real personal data, so the privacy rules around them are not optional.","Split brand and non-brand into separate campaigns so they stop competing with each other."],"action":"Open your account's location settings and confirm whether each active campaign is set to Presence or to Presence or interest — then fix any that don't match what the business can actually serve.","quiz":[{"q":"What does Observation mode do with an audience segment?","opts":["Restricts delivery to only that audience","Measures and allows bid adjustment without restricting delivery","Removes the audience from reporting entirely"],"correct":1,"explain":"Observation layers the segment on top for reporting and bidding, without excluding anyone outside it."},{"q":"In Performance Max, an audience signal mainly acts as...","opts":["A hard boundary the system can never cross","A starting point the system can expand beyond once it finds conversions","A setting with no real effect"],"correct":1,"explain":"Performance Max treats a signal as a head start, not a restriction."},{"q":"Why split brand and non-brand into separate Search campaigns?","opts":["So they stop competing with each other in reporting and in the auction","Because Google requires it","To reduce the total number of keywords"],"correct":0,"explain":"Splitting them prevents cheap brand clicks from flattering blended non-brand performance."},{"q":"What should you check before uploading a Customer Match list?","opts":["Nothing — any list can be uploaded immediately","Current consent, list size minimums and acceptable-use rules","Only that the file is a .csv"],"correct":1,"explain":"Customer Match runs on real personal data, so the privacy and eligibility rules apply."}],"related":[{"t":"Building Campaigns","d":"Where audiences meet keyword structure.","to":"m/campaigns"},{"t":"Glossary: Customer Match","d":"Definition and context.","to":"glossary/Customer Match"},{"t":"Glossary: Remarketing","d":"How it works.","to":"glossary/Remarketing"}]},{"id":"campaigns","n":4,"title":"Building Campaigns","sub":"Choosing the right campaign type and structuring it so the algorithm can learn.","intro":"A campaign's type decides what it's allowed to do; its structure decides how well the algorithm can learn inside that. Get the first choice wrong and no amount of optimization fixes it. Get the structure wrong and you'll spend weeks fighting noise that a cleaner setup would never have produced.","sections":[{"id":"choose","h":"Choosing the campaign type by goal","html":"<p>There's no universally 'best' campaign type — only the type that matches what you're actually trying to do. Search, Performance Max, Shopping, Demand Gen, Display, Video and App campaigns each exist for a different job, and picking by habit or by what a competitor runs is how accounts end up with a structure that fights itself.</p><p>Work backwards from the goal, not forwards from the format: decide whether you're capturing existing demand, creating new interest, or selling a product catalogue, and the campaign type mostly chooses itself from there.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":"c-obj"},{"id":"compare","h":"Search vs Performance Max vs Shopping vs Demand Gen","html":"<p>These four cover most beginner and intermediate accounts, and they're not interchangeable even when they can technically run the same keywords or audiences. Each trades control for reach differently.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":"c-abo"},{"id":"keywords","h":"Keyword research and match types","html":"<p>Start keyword research from your own site and real sales conversations, not a generic list — the words a customer used in a support ticket or a review usually outperform a thesaurus exercise. Group what you find by theme before you group by match type; themes become ad groups, and match type is a setting you apply within one.</p><p><strong>Broad match</strong> today leans on your landing page, other keywords in the account and Smart Bidding to guess intent rather than matching words literally, so it needs a bid strategy that can learn from conversion data to behave well. <strong>Phrase match</strong> targets searches containing your phrase's meaning with extra words allowed around it. <strong>Exact match</strong> is the tightest, targeting the same meaning as your keyword including close variants — still looser than the literal-match behaviour some advertisers remember from years ago.</p><p><strong>Negative keywords</strong> are what keep broad and phrase match honest: add them at the account level for things you never want and at the campaign level for things that are only wrong for that specific campaign. The <strong>search term</strong> report is the actual record of what people typed to trigger your ad — read it regularly, since it's the only reliable way to catch a match type drifting somewhere you didn't intend.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":null},{"id":"groups","h":"Structuring ad groups","html":"<p>An ad group should hold one tight theme — one product line, one service, one close family of intent — not fifty loosely related keywords dumped together. The narrower the theme, the easier it is to write ads that feel specifically written for that search, which is most of what ad relevance is actually measuring.</p><p>Split brand and non-brand into separate campaigns early, even in a small account. Brand terms are cheap and convert at a high rate almost by default, and will flatter blended performance numbers if mixed in with non-brand — making a weak non-brand campaign look fine when it isn't.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":null},{"id":"settings","h":"Network, location and schedule settings","html":"<p>A Search campaign's network settings can default to including <strong>Search Partners</strong> and, in some setups, Display expansion. Both can widen reach at the cost of control and reporting clarity; most focused accounts turn them off for Search and run Display as its own, separately measured campaign instead.</p><p>Location targeting again comes down to presence versus interest (covered in the audiences module), and schedule settings should usually stay broad until you have enough conversion data to see a real pattern by hour or day — narrowing a schedule before you have that data just removes volume you needed in order to learn.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":null},{"id":"checklist","h":"Campaign launch checklist","html":"<p>Treat this as a gate, not a suggestion. A campaign that launches with tracking broken or a theme-dump ad group costs real money to find out the hard way, when checking first costs nothing.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":"c-check"},{"id":"worked","h":"Structuring your first real campaign","html":"<p><strong>A local service business on Search.</strong> Two campaigns: Brand (your business name, tightly covered, Maximize clicks or a low Target CPA) and Non-brand (service and location combinations, split into ad groups by service type — 'emergency plumber', 'drain cleaning', 'boiler repair' — each with its own ads and, where it matters, its own landing page). The Primary conversion is usually the phone call or the form submit, not a page view.</p><p><strong>An ecommerce store on Shopping/Performance Max.</strong> One Shopping or ecommerce Performance Max campaign running from a clean Merchant Center feed, segmented into asset groups by product category rather than one group for the whole catalogue, alongside a separate brand Search campaign so brand searches aren't left entirely to the AI to decide on. The Primary conversion is Purchase, with conversion value passed through so ROAS-based bidding has something real to optimize.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":null}],"takeaways":["Group keywords into tight themes first; match type is a setting you apply within that theme, not the other way around.","Negative keywords and the search term report are how you keep broad and phrase match honest.","Split brand and non-brand into separate campaigns from day one.","Run the launch checklist as a gate — a broken tracking setup costs real money to discover after launch.","A local-service Search account and an ecommerce Shopping/PMax account are structured differently; match the pattern to your business, not a generic template."],"action":"Pick one product or service your business sells and sketch its ad group: write the keyword theme, three example search terms you'd want to match, and two you'd exclude as negatives.","quiz":[{"q":"What should decide how you group keywords into ad groups?","opts":["A tight theme, not match type","Alphabetical order","Cost per click"],"correct":0,"explain":"Themes become ad groups; match type is applied within a theme, not used to define one."},{"q":"What's the main job of a negative keyword?","opts":["To boost Ad Rank","To stop your ad showing for searches you don't want","To increase your maximum bid"],"correct":1,"explain":"Negatives filter out traffic you don't want to pay for."},{"q":"Why keep brand and non-brand in separate campaigns?","opts":["Brand keywords aren't allowed in the same campaign as non-brand","Cheap, high-converting brand clicks would otherwise flatter blended performance","Google requires a minimum of two campaigns per account"],"correct":1,"explain":"Mixing them hides true non-brand performance behind easy brand wins."},{"q":"For an ecommerce Shopping/PMax campaign, what's usually the Primary conversion?","opts":["Page view","Purchase, with conversion value passed through","Add to cart"],"correct":1,"explain":"Purchase with value carried through is what ROAS-based bidding needs to optimize toward."}],"related":[{"t":"Audiences & Targeting","d":"How segments layer onto the structure you just built.","to":"m/audiences"},{"t":"Glossary: Negative keyword","d":"Definition and context.","to":"glossary/Negative keyword"},{"t":"Budgets, Bidding & the Auction","d":"Choosing how those campaigns bid.","to":"m/bidding"}]},{"id":"bidding","n":5,"title":"Budgets, Bidding & the Auction","sub":"Bid strategies, the learning period, and why patience is a skill.","intro":"Structure decides what the algorithm has to work with; bidding decides how aggressively and toward what goal it uses it. Most accounts that plateau aren't short on structure — they're short on patience, changing strategy faster than any strategy can prove itself.","sections":[{"id":"bid","h":"Bid strategies available","html":"<p>Manual CPC is the only strategy where you set each bid directly; it gives full control but no automated help, and is mostly a fallback for very low-conversion accounts or a baseline to compare automation against. Maximize clicks spends your budget to get as many clicks as possible, which is a volume strategy, not an efficiency one — useful for early traffic, not for an account trying to hit a cost target.</p><p><strong>Target CPA</strong> and <strong>Target ROAS</strong> are the value-based automated strategies most accounts graduate to once they have real conversion history. Google has presented these in different ways over time, sometimes as standalone strategies and sometimes as an optional target inside Maximize conversions or Maximize conversion value, so you may see either naming in your account.</p><p><strong>Target impression share</strong> bids to win a chosen share of auctions at a chosen position, which is a visibility strategy rather than an efficiency one — a reasonable choice for a brand campaign where simply being seen in position one matters more than hitting a strict cost target.</p>","callout":{"k":"info","t":"Bid strategy names and how they're grouped in the interface have changed more than once. Confirm the current names and settings in your account against the Google Ads Help Centre before choosing one."},"figure":null,"widget":"b-bid"},{"id":"budget","h":"Budgets","html":"<p>A daily budget is an average, not a hard daily cap. Google can spend up to roughly double your daily budget on a day of higher opportunity, but won't exceed what your monthly billing cycle would add up to at that average rate — so a $50-a-day budget might see a $90 day, but would not be charged more than about $1,520 (30.4 × $50) in a billing month.</p><p><strong>Shared budgets</strong> let several campaigns draw from one pool instead of each holding its own fixed amount, useful when campaigns have overlapping value and you'd rather let the system move spend to whichever is performing — though it makes it harder to guarantee any single campaign a minimum amount of spend.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":null},{"id":"learning","h":"The learning period","html":"<p>Any time you create a new campaign, switch bid strategy, or make a significant edit to targeting or budget, the bid strategy enters a <strong>learning period</strong> — a stretch where performance is typically more erratic than usual while the system gathers enough fresh conversion data to optimize reliably again. This isn't a malfunction; it's the system relearning after the ground shifted under it.</p><p>The practical implication is patience: resist the urge to change a target, pause a keyword, or swap strategies every few days while a campaign is learning, since each change can reset the clock. Give a change roughly one to two weeks, or enough conversions to be meaningful, before judging whether it worked.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":"b-learn"},{"id":"scale","h":"Scaling without breaking what works","html":"<p>The accounts that scale successfully change one variable at a time and in modest steps — a 15–20% budget increase, not a doubling overnight; a target nudged, not reset. Big, simultaneous changes make it impossible to tell afterward which change caused which result, and tend to reset the learning period right as the extra budget most needs stable bidding.</p><p><strong>Experiments</strong> let you test a change — a new bid strategy, a different budget, a creative variant — against a held-out split of traffic before committing the whole campaign to it, which is a safer way to learn whether a change actually helps than guessing from a before/after comparison that could just as easily reflect seasonality.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":null},{"id":"portfolio","h":"Portfolio bid strategies","html":"<p>A portfolio bid strategy applies one Target CPA or Target ROAS across several campaigns at once, letting the system move budget and effort toward whichever campaign is converting best at that moment rather than optimizing each campaign in isolation. It usually needs more combined conversion volume to work well than a single-campaign strategy, so it fits established accounts with several related campaigns more than a brand-new one.</p><p>The trade-off is the same one shared budgets carry: less guarantee that any individual campaign in the portfolio gets a fixed amount of attention, in exchange for the pooled strategy having more data to learn from overall.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":null}],"takeaways":["A daily budget is an average; Google can spend up to roughly double it on a strong day, never more than the monthly cap implies.","Target CPA and Target ROAS have been offered both as standalone strategies and as targets inside Maximize conversions or Maximize conversion value, so expect the naming to vary.","A learning period follows any significant change; judge results after it settles, not during it.","Scale in modest, one-variable-at-a-time steps, and use Experiments to test before committing fully.","Portfolio strategies pool several campaigns under one target, trading per-campaign guarantees for more shared data."],"action":"Open your most active campaign's change history and count how many times its bid strategy or targets changed in the last 30 days — if it's more than once or twice, that's likely why performance looks erratic.","quiz":[{"q":"What happens if your daily budget is $50 and Google sees a strong day?","opts":["It can spend up to roughly double, within the monthly cap","It is hard-capped at exactly $50","It automatically raises your monthly cap"],"correct":0,"explain":"A daily budget is an average; Google can overspend on a given day but not beyond the monthly implied cap."},{"q":"What triggers a new learning period?","opts":["Leaving a campaign untouched for a month","A significant edit to bid strategy, targeting or budget","Checking your reports more than once a day"],"correct":1,"explain":"Significant edits reset how much the system has learned under current conditions."},{"q":"What's the safer way to test a new bid strategy?","opts":["Switch the whole campaign and compare before/after","Run it as an Experiment against a held-out split of traffic","Change it and the budget and the targeting all at once"],"correct":1,"explain":"Experiments isolate the one variable so you can tell what actually caused the result."},{"q":"What's the main trade-off of a portfolio bid strategy?","opts":["It costs extra to use","Less guarantee of spend for any one campaign, in exchange for more shared data","It only works with Manual CPC"],"correct":1,"explain":"Pooling campaigns under one target trades individual guarantees for combined learning."}],"related":[{"t":"Foundations","d":"Revisit how Ad Rank and the auction work.","to":"m/foundations"},{"t":"Glossary: Target CPA","d":"Definition and context.","to":"glossary/Target CPA"},{"t":"Troubleshooting & Optimization","d":"What to check when a bid strategy change goes wrong.","to":"m/troubleshooting"}]},{"id":"creative","n":6,"title":"Creative & Ad Copy","sub":"Assets are now the main lever you control.","intro":"As bidding and matching have shifted to automation, the clearest lever left in your hands is the creative itself — the headlines, descriptions and assets that make up what a person actually sees. This module is about using that lever deliberately instead of defaulting to whatever's fastest to write.","sections":[{"id":"rsa","h":"Anatomy of a Responsive Search Ad","html":"<p>A Responsive Search Ad holds up to 15 headlines (30 characters each) and up to 4 descriptions (90 characters each); Google assembles combinations of these automatically and learns which pairings perform best for a given search. You need a minimum of three headlines and two descriptions to publish, but more genuinely different options give the system more combinations worth learning from — near-duplicate headlines don't.</p><p><strong>Pinning</strong> locks a specific headline or description to a specific position, guaranteeing it shows — but pinning everything defeats the point of a responsive format, since it removes the combinations Google would otherwise test. <strong>Ad strength</strong>, rated Poor to Excellent, is a guide to variety and relevance, not a target to game: a Poor-rated ad with genuinely strong, specific copy can still outperform an Excellent-rated ad stuffed with generic keyword variations.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":null},{"id":"copy","h":"A copywriting framework that holds up","html":"<p>A framework that holds up across most industries: claim, reason, outcome. State what you offer plainly (claim), give one credible reason to believe it (reason — a number, a guarantee, a credential), then say what the person gets (outcome), ideally in language close to what they'd use to describe the result themselves.</p><p>Work the keyword theme naturally into at least a couple of headlines — it reinforces relevance and often gets bolded in the result — alongside a differentiator, some form of proof, and a clear, specific call to action. 'Get a free quote today' beats 'Learn more' because it tells the person exactly what happens next.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":null},{"id":"assets","h":"Ad assets and extensions","html":"<p><strong>Assets</strong> (the current name for what used to be called extensions) are the parts of an ad beyond headlines and descriptions: sitelinks, callouts, structured snippets, images, a call asset, a lead form asset, price assets, promotion assets, and your business name and logo. Assets aren't optional polish — they're part of Ad Rank, and an ad with strong, relevant assets can win a better position than a higher bid with none.</p><p>Write assets as specifically as you write headlines. A callout that just says 'Quality service' says nothing a competitor couldn't also claim; '20 years in business' or 'Same-day response' says something concrete.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":null},{"id":"pmax-creative","h":"Performance Max asset groups and Demand Gen creative","html":"<p>Performance Max organizes creative into <strong>asset groups</strong> rather than ads — each group holds its own images, logos, video, headlines and descriptions, built around one theme, audience and set of landing pages, similar in spirit to how an ad group organizes a Search campaign. Give each asset group genuine variety: multiple aspect ratios of images, at least one video, and headlines that don't all say the same thing in slightly different words.</p><p>Demand Gen leans even more on visual quality, since its placements — YouTube, Discover, Gmail — are browsed rather than searched. Supply genuinely native-feeling creative for each surface rather than one repurposed banner ad, and treat it as awareness and consideration content, not a hard sales pitch.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":null},{"id":"testing","h":"Creative testing discipline","html":"<p>Creative testing works the same way any good test does: change one variable, keep everything else constant, and let it run long enough to gather a meaningful amount of data before judging it. Testing two RSAs with completely different headline sets, images and tones at once tells you which ad won, not why — testing one new headline against the old set in an otherwise-identical ad tells you something you can actually act on next time.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":"cr-test"},{"id":"sig","h":"Is the test result real?","html":"<p>Before declaring a test result real, run the plain-English check: is the gap between variants bigger than the kind of swing you'd expect from a handful of extra clicks landing one way instead of the other? A 2% difference in conversion rate off 40 clicks each is well within normal noise; the same gap off 4,000 clicks each is a result worth acting on. This isn't a substitute for a proper significance calculation, just a sanity check against declaring a winner too early.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":"cr-split"},{"id":"types","h":"Creative types, at a glance","html":"<p>Different creative types suit different jobs: RSAs for intent-driven Search, PMax asset groups for cross-channel automated delivery, Demand Gen creative for discovery placements, and Display image and HTML5 ads for the Display Network specifically. Knowing which type you're building for before you start writing avoids the common mistake of writing one generic ad and trying to force-fit it everywhere.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":"cr-types"},{"id":"policy","h":"Policy-safe copy","html":"<p>Google's ad policies prohibit misleading claims and excessive capitalisation or punctuation, and carry specific, sometimes region-specific restrictions around categories like healthcare, financial services, alcohol and gambling. Trademark use in ad copy has its own rules too, separate from keyword bidding on a competitor's name — bidding on a term and using it in visible ad text are judged differently.</p>","callout":{"k":"warn","t":"Advertising policy is reviewed and updated often, and restricted categories vary by country. Check the current Google Ads policy centre for your specific industry and region before publishing, not this course."},"figure":null,"widget":null}],"takeaways":["15 headlines, 4 descriptions — but genuinely different options matter more than hitting the maximum.","Ad strength is a guide to variety, not a target to chase at the expense of specific, honest copy.","Assets are part of Ad Rank, not optional decoration.","Performance Max creative lives in asset groups, built around one theme, audience and landing page at a time.","Change one variable at a time when testing creative, and check that a result is bigger than ordinary noise before acting on it."],"action":"Pull up your best-performing ad group and count genuinely distinct headlines, not just reworded near-duplicates. Write three new ones that are actually different in angle, not just phrasing.","quiz":[{"q":"What's the minimum number of headlines needed to publish an RSA?","opts":["One","Three","Fifteen"],"correct":1,"explain":"Three headlines and two descriptions are the minimum to publish."},{"q":"What does pinning a headline do?","opts":["Locks it to a specific position so it always shows there","Automatically improves Ad Strength","Deletes the other headlines"],"correct":0,"explain":"Pinning guarantees a position but can reduce the combinations being tested."},{"q":"In Performance Max, creative is organized into...","opts":["Ad groups, same as Search","Asset groups, built around one theme at a time","A single global creative pool with no structure"],"correct":1,"explain":"Asset groups are PMax's version of an ad group — one theme, audience and landing page set at a time."},{"q":"A 2% conversion-rate gap shows up after only 40 clicks per variant. What should you conclude?","opts":["The variant with the higher rate is the clear winner — ship it","That gap could easily be ordinary noise at that sample size","Conversion rate doesn't matter in creative testing"],"correct":1,"explain":"At such a small sample, a 2-point gap is well within normal variation."}],"related":[{"t":"Building Campaigns","d":"Where these ads and assets get deployed.","to":"m/campaigns"},{"t":"Glossary: Responsive Search Ad","d":"Definition and context.","to":"glossary/Responsive Search Ad"},{"t":"Tracking, Metrics & Analytics","d":"Reading what a creative test actually tells you.","to":"m/tracking"}]},{"id":"tracking","n":7,"title":"Tracking, Metrics & Analytics","sub":"Reading the numbers correctly and knowing which ones matter.","intro":"Every number in a Google Ads report is downstream of a choice you made earlier — a conversion action, an attribution setting, a bid strategy. This module is about reading those numbers as links in a chain rather than as isolated facts, so a change in one tells you something useful about the others.","sections":[{"id":"metrics","h":"Core metrics","html":"<p><strong>Impressions</strong> count how often your ad was shown; <strong>clicks</strong> count how often someone clicked it; <strong>CTR</strong> is clicks divided by impressions. <strong>Avg. CPC</strong> is what you actually paid per click on average. None of these alone tells you whether the campaign is working — they describe traffic, not outcomes.</p><p><strong>Conversions</strong>, conversion rate (conversions ÷ clicks) and <strong>CPA</strong> (cost ÷ conversions) start answering the outcome question; conversion value and <strong>ROAS</strong> (value ÷ cost) answer it in money terms when you're tracking value. <strong>Impression share</strong> and its two 'lost' variants — lost to budget, lost to rank — explain how much eligible demand you're missing and why, often the fastest diagnostic when volume looks low.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":null},{"id":"chain","h":"The metric chain","html":"<p>These numbers aren't independent facts; they're links in one chain, and each is derived from the ones before it: CPC = cost ÷ clicks. Conversion rate = conversions ÷ clicks. CPA = CPC ÷ conversion rate, which is the same as cost ÷ conversions. ROAS = conversion value ÷ cost. Seeing them this way turns 'my CPA went up' from a mystery into an arithmetic question — did CPC rise, did conversion rate fall, or both?</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":"t-calc"},{"id":"quality","h":"Quality signals","html":"<p><strong>Quality Score</strong>, visible as a 1–10 diagnostic in the keyword columns, is built from expected CTR, ad relevance and landing page experience — the same three ingredients that feed into Ad Rank. Treat it as a diagnostic that tells you where a problem might be, not a KPI to chase directly: improving the three components usually raises the number anyway, while chasing the number itself by guessing usually doesn't fix the components.</p><p>A keyword with consistently Below Average landing page experience is telling you something concrete and fixable — the page doesn't match what the ad promised, loads slowly, or doesn't work well on mobile — which is a better use of the signal than treating the single digit as a report card.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":null},{"id":"attribution","h":"Attribution","html":"<p><strong>Data-driven attribution</strong> is Google Ads' default model, distributing credit for a conversion across the different ads and clicks that contributed to it, based on patterns observed in your own account's data, rather than giving full credit to the last click. Conversion windows — how long after a click or view a conversion can still be counted — and conversion lag — how long conversions typically take to arrive after the click — both affect how complete your reporting looks in the first few days after a campaign runs.</p><p><strong>View-through conversions</strong> count people who saw but didn't click an ad, mostly on Display and video, and later converted — directionally useful, but genuinely harder to act on with precision than a click-based conversion, since 'saw an ad' is a much weaker, noisier signal of causation than 'clicked an ad'.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":"t-attr"},{"id":"recon","h":"Reconciling Google Ads numbers with GA4, Shopify/CRM revenue","html":"<p>It's normal, not alarming, for Google Ads' reported conversions and revenue to differ from GA4's or from your store or CRM's own numbers — different attribution windows, different attribution models, and different definitions of what counts as a conversion all produce different totals from the same underlying events. The question worth asking isn't 'why don't these match exactly,' it's 'is the gap stable and explainable, or growing and unexplained.'</p><p>A stable, moderate gap you understand — say, Google Ads showing slightly fewer conversions than Shopify because of a tighter attribution window — is normal. A gap that's growing, or one you can't explain by attribution settings alone, usually points to a tracking problem worth chasing down with the troubleshooting steps in the next module.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":null},{"id":"terms","h":"The search terms report and how to act on it","html":"<p>The <strong>search term</strong> report is the single most underused tool in most accounts: it's the literal record of what people typed before your ad showed, which is the only direct evidence of how your match types are actually behaving in practice. Reviewing it regularly — weekly for an active account — surfaces both negative-keyword candidates and new keyword ideas you hadn't explicitly targeted.</p><p>Read it with a question in mind, not just a skim: for a given ad group, do the search terms actually match the theme that ad group was built around? Drift here, more than almost any other single signal, is what separates an account quietly wasting budget from one that's compounding what's working.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":"t-diag"}],"takeaways":["Impressions, clicks and CTR describe traffic; conversions, CPA and ROAS describe outcomes — don't mistake one for the other.","CPA and ROAS are arithmetic, not mysteries: they're built from CPC and conversion rate.","Quality Score is a diagnostic of the same three things that build Ad Rank, not a target to chase directly.","A gap between Google Ads and GA4 or CRM revenue is normal; a growing or unexplained gap is a tracking problem.","The search term report is the most direct evidence of how your match types are actually behaving."],"action":"Pull your account's search term report from the last 30 days and find at least three searches that don't match the ad group's theme — add them as negatives.","quiz":[{"q":"What does CPA equal?","opts":["Clicks ÷ impressions","Cost ÷ conversions","Conversion value ÷ cost"],"correct":1,"explain":"CPA is cost divided by conversions — what each conversion cost you on average."},{"q":"What is Google Ads' default attribution model?","opts":["Last click only","Data-driven attribution, which distributes credit across contributing touchpoints","First click only"],"correct":1,"explain":"Data-driven attribution is the default, learned from your own account's conversion paths."},{"q":"A keyword shows consistently Below Average landing page experience. What does that suggest?","opts":["Lower your bid immediately","The landing page likely doesn't match the ad's promise or loads poorly","Nothing — the metric is cosmetic"],"correct":1,"explain":"Landing page experience is a real diagnostic of a real, fixable problem."},{"q":"What should concern you about a gap between Google Ads and GA4 conversion numbers?","opts":["Any gap at all, however small","A gap that's growing or can't be explained by attribution settings","Only gaps larger than 50%"],"correct":1,"explain":"A stable, explainable gap is normal; a growing or unexplained one points to a tracking issue."}],"related":[{"t":"Troubleshooting & Optimization","d":"What to do when these numbers look wrong.","to":"m/troubleshooting"},{"t":"Glossary: Data-driven attribution","d":"Definition and context.","to":"glossary/Data-driven attribution"},{"t":"Budgets, Bidding & the Auction","d":"How bid strategies use this data.","to":"m/bidding"}]},{"id":"troubleshooting","n":8,"title":"Troubleshooting & Optimization","sub":"A decision tree for when something breaks.","intro":"When results change, the instinct is to start pulling levers. The discipline is to diagnose first, in an order that rules out the cheap, common causes before the rare, expensive ones. This module gives you that order, the checks inside each step, and a weekly routine that improves an account without damaging it.","sections":[{"id":"order","h":"The diagnostic order: tracking first","html":"<p>Most account problems come from a short list of causes, and they are cheapest to rule out in a fixed order: <strong>tracking and measurement</strong> first, then <strong>account status and policy</strong>, then <strong>budget and bids</strong>, then <strong>targeting and matching</strong>, then <strong>ads and landing pages</strong>, and only last, outside factors such as seasonality and competitors.</p><p>Tracking comes first because a broken tag looks exactly like a performance collapse, and no bid change can fix it. Worse, <strong>Smart Bidding</strong> learns from whatever it is told, so if conversions quietly stop recording, the system can start bidding down on the very traffic that was working. Use the tree below to find your starting point, then read the matching section.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":"tr-tree"},{"id":"tracking","h":"Tracking problems: conversions missing or doubled","html":"<p>Start in the conversion actions list. A status such as 'No recent conversions' or 'Unverified' next to an action that used to record is your first clue. Then use Tag Assistant, or the preview mode in Google Tag Manager, to load the page and confirm that the <strong>Google tag</strong> fires on the key event and that the <strong>gclid</strong> survives the journey from ad click to conversion page.</p><p>Three failures cause most tracking trouble. The first is a changed website: a theme update, a new checkout or a page-builder change removed or moved the tag. The second is lost click data: redirects, URL rewriting or a cookie banner stop the gclid and cookies from being kept, so check that <strong>Consent Mode</strong> and <strong>Enhanced conversions</strong> are still configured. The third is double counting: the same conversion is fired by two tags, for example one hard-coded and one in Tag Manager, so numbers jump for no reason.</p><p>Also check the counting setting. For purchases, counting every conversion makes sense because each sale is real. For lead forms, counting one per click usually fits better, so a person who submits twice isn't counted as two leads. And remember <strong>conversion lag</strong>: conversions can arrive days after the click, so the most recent days always look worse than they eventually will.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":null},{"id":"status","h":"Account, billing and policy problems","html":"<p>If ads stop showing altogether, check status before strategy. The campaigns table shows statuses such as Eligible, Limited, Under review or Disapproved, and the Policy manager lists every ad or asset that has been flagged, with the reason. A declined payment method or a billing problem can also pause delivery across the whole account.</p><p>For a disapproval, read the exact reason, fix the ad or the landing page rather than resubmitting the same thing, and appeal only if you believe the decision was wrong. Never open a new account to get around a suspension. Google treats that as circumventing its systems, and it makes recovery harder. For anything account-level, use the official appeal route in the Help Centre.</p>","callout":{"k":"warn","t":"Policy names, statuses and appeal steps change. Confirm the current process in the Google Ads Help Centre before acting on a suspension or a repeated disapproval."},"figure":null,"widget":null},{"id":"delivery","h":"Low delivery: few impressions or low spend","html":"<p>When an account isn't spending, the cause is usually one of four things: ads or keywords aren't approved yet, the <strong>bid strategy</strong> or bids are too low to win auctions, targeting is too narrow (tight location, small audience, many exclusions), or demand is simply small. The <strong>impression share</strong> columns tell you which: lost to budget means money is the limit, and lost to rank means your Ad Rank is too low to win more auctions.</p><p>A new campaign or a recent change also runs through the <strong>learning period</strong>, when delivery can be uneven. If the status says Learning, wait before changing anything else. If the campaign has been stable for weeks and spend is still low, widen one thing at a time: broaden matching on a proven theme, add a related ad group, or raise the target slightly.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":null},{"id":"cost","h":"Rising costs and falling returns","html":"<p>Because CPA equals CPC divided by conversion rate, a rising <strong>CPA</strong> has two possible sources, and the fix differs. If <strong>CPC</strong> rose, look at competition in the <strong>Auction Insights</strong> report, at match drift in the <strong>search term</strong> report, and at falling quality signals. If conversion rate fell, look at the landing page, the offer, form changes, device mix, and tracking again.</p><p>Compare equal periods, ideally the same weekdays, and segment by device, network, location and hour before concluding anything. Then read the <strong>change history</strong> for edits that line up with the date the numbers moved. Seasonality and one-off events, such as a sale or a stock-out, explain more swings than most people expect.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":null},{"id":"landing","h":"When the problem is the landing page","html":"<p>If clicks are healthy and conversions aren't, and the search terms match what you sell, the landing page is the next suspect. Check that it loads quickly on a phone, that the headline repeats the promise of the ad, that the next step is obvious, and that the form or checkout doesn't ask for more than it needs.</p><p>Confirm that what the ad promises, such as a price or an offer, is true on the page. A mismatch hurts conversions and drags down <strong>landing page experience</strong>. Test one change at a time, using the same discipline as the creative testing in Module 6.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":null},{"id":"fatigue","h":"Creative and asset fatigue","html":"<p>Fatigue shows up as a slow slide rather than a break: click-through rate drifts down, cost per click creeps up, and conversion volume softens while nothing else in the account has changed. It is most visible on Demand Gen, Display and video, where the same people see the same creative repeatedly, and less common on Search, where each query is a fresh moment of intent.</p><p>Check that fatigue is the cause before reacting: confirm tracking is healthy, compare asset-level performance over a few weeks, and look at frequency where it is reported. The fix is new angles, not cosmetic tweaks, so add fresh headlines, images or video to the same asset group instead of rebuilding it, and retire only the assets that are clearly weakest.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":null},{"id":"routine","h":"A weekly routine that doesn't cause damage","html":"<p>A good optimization routine is boring and bounded. Each week: scan for disapprovals and billing alerts, review the search terms and add negatives, check budgets and impression share lost to budget, look at asset and ad performance for clear losers, and write down what you changed and why. Each month, step back to trends, device and location splits, and landing page performance.</p><p>Treat the <strong>Optimization score</strong> and the Recommendations page as a source of ideas, not a to-do list. Some suggestions fit your goals and others mainly increase spend. Read each one, apply what matches your strategy, dismiss the rest, and review any auto-apply settings so nothing changes without your knowledge. A short change log also makes the next diagnosis much faster.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":null}],"takeaways":["Diagnose in a fixed order: tracking, status and policy, budget and bids, targeting, ads and pages, then outside factors.","A tracking failure looks like a performance collapse, and Smart Bidding can learn the wrong lesson from it.","CPA is CPC divided by conversion rate, so ask which of the two moved.","Read the change history before blaming the algorithm.","Never evade a suspension with a new account; use the official appeal route.","Recommendations are ideas to evaluate, not instructions to obey."],"action":"Pick one account and write your own diagnostic checklist in the order from this module. Then walk the most recent dip in results through it, and note which step explained it.","quiz":[{"q":"Conversions suddenly drop while clicks look normal. What do you check first?","opts":["Raise bids","Whether tracking still fires and records conversions","Rewrite the headlines"],"correct":1,"explain":"A broken tag looks like a performance collapse, so tracking is ruled out before anything else."},{"q":"Which counting setting usually suits a lead form?","opts":["Every conversion","One conversion per click","Neither can be used for leads"],"correct":1,"explain":"Counting one per click stops a person who submits twice from being counted as two leads."},{"q":"CPA has climbed. Which two numbers do you decompose it into?","opts":["CPC and conversion rate","Impressions and CTR","Budget and bid strategy"],"correct":0,"explain":"CPA equals CPC divided by conversion rate, so one or both of those moved."},{"q":"Numbers changed on a particular date for no obvious reason. What is a good next step?","opts":["Switch the bid strategy","Review the change history around that date","Double the budget"],"correct":1,"explain":"The change history shows what was edited, when and by whom, which often explains the shift."}],"related":[{"t":"Tracking, Metrics & Analytics","d":"The metric chain behind every diagnosis.","to":"m/tracking"},{"t":"Glossary: Change history","d":"What the log records.","to":"glossary/Change history"},{"t":"Scaling & Strategy","d":"What to do once the account is healthy.","to":"m/scaling"}]},{"id":"scaling","n":9,"title":"Scaling & Strategy","sub":"Growing a winning account without breaking it.","intro":"Scaling is not turning a budget dial. It is the discipline of growing spend only while returns hold, and of knowing what the numbers must be for growth to stay profitable. This module covers when to scale, how to do it without breaking learning, and the business economics behind every target.","sections":[{"id":"when","h":"Is the account ready to scale?","html":"<p>Scale something that works, not something you hope will work. Good signs: conversions are tracked and trusted, CPA or ROAS has been stable for several weeks, there is enough conversion volume for <strong>Smart Bidding</strong> to learn from, and the <strong>impression share</strong> report shows you losing auctions to budget rather than to rank. If you are losing to rank, more budget won't help; quality and bids will.</p><p>Scaling a leaky bucket only makes the leak bigger. If tracking is doubtful or the landing page converts poorly, fix that first, using the order from Module 8. The cheapest growth is usually a better conversion rate, because it lowers CPA on every click you already buy.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":null},{"id":"budget","h":"Scaling budget in steps","html":"<p>The reliable method is incremental. Raise the budget by around 15 to 20 percent, let the campaign run a full cycle (often one to two weeks, or enough conversions to judge), check CPA or ROAS against your target, and repeat only if it held. Large jumps tend to restart the <strong>learning period</strong>, and they make it impossible to tell whether a worse result came from the budget or from something else.</p><p>Use the planner to see how long a ramp takes. Notice how many steps even a modest goal needs; this is why scaling is a patience skill. If time matters, add budget through a second, similar campaign or test the change as an <strong>Experiment</strong>, rather than shocking a stable campaign.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":"sc-calc"},{"id":"pace","h":"Pacing a monthly budget","html":"<p>A daily budget is an average. Google can spend up to about twice the daily amount on a busy day, and over a billing month it does not charge more than roughly 30.4 times your average daily budget. That makes monthly <strong>pacing</strong> a check you run yourself: compare spend to date with the share of the month that has passed, and project where you will land.</p><p>If you are pacing high, don't panic-cut on one day; look at whether the extra spend bought conversions at a good CPA. If you are pacing low, find the reason (lost to rank, narrow targeting, pending approvals) before raising the budget, which only helps when budget is the constraint.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":"sc-pace"},{"id":"expand","h":"Four ways to grow","html":"<p>Growth comes from four places, and the order matters. <strong>More of what works</strong>: more budget and better impression share on campaigns that already hit target. <strong>New demand</strong>: new keyword themes, new campaign types such as Shopping, Performance Max or Demand Gen, new audiences. <strong>New markets</strong>: new locations, languages or products. <strong>Higher value per customer</strong>: a better conversion rate, a higher order value, repeat purchase, or value-based bidding fed with real revenue or lead-quality data.</p><p>Add one at a time and give each its own measurement window. If you launch three of these at once, you will see the total change but won't learn which of them produced it.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":null},{"id":"cadence","h":"An optimization cadence","html":"<p>Scaling works better inside a rhythm than in bursts of activity. Daily checks catch breakages, weekly work improves quality, monthly reviews change direction, and quarterly reviews change strategy. Choose a tab to see the cadence this course recommends.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":"sc-cadence"},{"id":"returns","h":"Diminishing returns and incrementality","html":"<p>Each extra unit of spend buys slightly worse traffic than the one before. That is why <strong>marginal CPA</strong>, the cost of the next increment of conversions, is always higher than the average CPA in the report. A campaign can show an acceptable average CPA while the last 20 percent of its spend loses money.</p><p>A related question is <strong>incrementality</strong>: how many of those conversions would have happened anyway? Brand search and remarketing often look excellent in reports partly because they capture people who were already on their way. Experiments and holdout tests, for example pausing a campaign in one region, are the honest way to find out.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":null},{"id":"walk","h":"When to walk away from an account or offer","html":"<p>Scaling is not always the right answer. Some offers cannot make money at any realistic cost per acquisition: the margin is too thin, the landing page cannot convert, the product is not what people searching want, or the client will not fix a tracking problem that blinds every bid strategy. Spending more on a structurally broken offer only loses money faster.</p><p>Before walking away, run a simple check: is tracking accurate, has the offer been tested with at least two genuinely different angles, and do the unit economics work on paper at the cost per acquisition you are seeing? If the answers stay no after a fair, documented effort, say so plainly, propose what would need to change, and end the engagement professionally rather than keep billing for work that cannot succeed.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":null},{"id":"economics","h":"Unit economics: the numbers behind every decision","html":"<p>Every target should come from business economics, not from a benchmark. Start with gross margin per sale. <strong>Break-even ROAS</strong> equals 1 divided by gross margin: at a 40 percent margin you need 2.5x just to cover the product and the ad cost, before overheads. Break-even CPA is the gross profit per conversion.</p><p>For leads, work backwards: if one lead in five becomes a customer worth 500 in profit, a lead is worth about 100, and a CPA above that loses money. Set targets with room above break-even for overheads and profit, and revisit them when margins, prices or lead quality change. When the true value of a lead is only known later, send it back with <strong>Offline conversion import</strong> so bidding optimizes for quality, not just volume.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":null}],"takeaways":["Scale only a campaign that is stable, tracked and limited by budget rather than rank.","Raise budgets in steps of roughly 15 to 20 percent and judge each one before the next.","A daily budget is an average; monthly pacing is something you check yourself.","Grow one lever at a time so you can learn what worked.","Marginal CPA is higher than average CPA, so the last pound or dollar is the hardest to justify.","Break-even ROAS is 1 divided by gross margin; targets start there."],"action":"Take one campaign and calculate its break-even ROAS or CPA from real margin figures. Then write down its current CPA or ROAS and the next two budget steps you would take, with the date you will judge each.","quiz":[{"q":"What is the break-even ROAS at a 40% gross margin?","opts":["1.4x","2.5x","4.0x"],"correct":1,"explain":"Break-even ROAS is 1 divided by gross margin, and 1 ÷ 0.4 is 2.5."},{"q":"Which is the best sign an account is ready to scale?","opts":["One excellent day","Stable CPA for several weeks and impression share lost to budget","A campaign launched yesterday"],"correct":1,"explain":"Stable results plus budget as the real constraint mean extra spend can be put to use."},{"q":"What does marginal CPA describe?","opts":["The cost of the next increment of conversions you buy","The average CPA across the account","The CPA on brand terms only"],"correct":0,"explain":"Marginal CPA is usually higher than the average CPA as spend grows."},{"q":"Why raise a budget in small steps?","opts":["Google refuses large changes","Big jumps tend to restart learning and hide the cause of any change","Small steps are cheaper per click"],"correct":1,"explain":"Smaller steps keep bidding stable and make cause and effect readable."}],"related":[{"t":"Budgets, Bidding & the Auction","d":"The learning period and bid strategies.","to":"m/bidding"},{"t":"Glossary: Break-even ROAS","d":"How the figure is calculated.","to":"glossary/Break-even ROAS"},{"t":"Troubleshooting & Optimization","d":"What to do when scaling goes wrong.","to":"m/troubleshooting"}]},{"id":"agency","n":10,"title":"Agency Operations & Client Management","sub":"Access, ownership, billing and reporting for client work.","intro":"Running campaigns for clients is a different job from running your own. The Google Ads skills are the same, but the risk is not: access, ownership and money belong to someone else. The agencies that last are the ones that set those foundations up clearly before the first campaign launches.","sections":[{"id":"access","h":"Access: how you get into a client account","html":"<p>Work through your own <strong>Manager account</strong> and ask the client to accept a link request from it, or have the client invite your email, rather than ever sharing a password. Access through a manager link is traceable, can be removed cleanly in either direction, and doesn't depend on one person's login. Clients should keep an admin user on their own account at all times.</p><p>Google Ads offers several access levels, from read-only and email-only through to standard and admin. Give each person the least that lets them do their job. <strong>Standard access</strong> is usually enough for day-to-day campaign work; ask for <strong>Admin access</strong> only where you genuinely need to manage users. Record who has access in your onboarding notes and review it whenever someone leaves.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":null},{"id":"ownership","h":"Asset ownership","html":"<p>The single most important rule in agency work is that the client owns their assets. The ad account, its history, the billing profile, the analytics property, the tag container, the product feed and the domain are the client's business infrastructure. If you create them under your own login or card, a dispute or a departure can strand the client or hold them hostage, and either outcome damages your reputation.</p><p>The table sets out a sensible default. A contract can vary it, but a variation should be deliberate and written down.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":"ag-own"},{"id":"billing","h":"Billing and fees","html":"<p>Billing has two layers. <strong>Ad spend</strong> is what Google charges for clicks; the management fee is what you charge for your work. Keep them separate on paper. The cleanest setup is the client paying Google directly with their own payment method and paying you a fee on top. If you pay Google and re-bill the client, you are extending credit and carrying risk, so only do it under a clear written agreement.</p><p>Common fee structures are a flat monthly retainer, a percentage of ad spend, a hybrid of the two, or a fee tied to results. Each has trade-offs: a percentage rewards spending more, a flat fee rewards efficiency but needs firm scope limits, and performance fees need very clear definitions of what is measured. Larger accounts may qualify for <strong>Invoiced billing</strong>, which is subject to Google's eligibility approval, so confirm the current requirements in the Help Centre.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":null},{"id":"onboarding","h":"Client onboarding","html":"<p>A structured onboarding turns the first month from chaos into a plan. Step through the sequence below and treat each step as something you file: notes from the discovery call, the access record, the audit, the baseline sheet, the plan and the reporting agreement.</p><p>Don't skip the audit. Taking over an account with broken tracking means you will be judged on numbers you can't trust. Record baselines before you change anything, so later improvements are provable, and agree in writing what success looks like.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":"ag-onb"},{"id":"system","h":"The agency operating system","html":"<p>An agency operating system is simply the set of repeatable routines that keep your work consistent whether you have two clients or twenty. It doesn't need special software; it needs documents and habits. Think of it as seven layers.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":null},{"id":"reporting","h":"Reporting clients actually read","html":"<p>Clients don't want every metric. They want to know whether their money is working and what you will do next. Lead with outcomes tied to the goals you agreed (leads, sales, revenue, CPA or ROAS), then explain the main drivers, then list the actions for the coming period. One page beats ten.</p><p>Connect Google Ads to a reporting tool such as <strong>Looker Studio</strong> for a live dashboard, but keep a short written summary beside it, because charts don't explain themselves. Report misses honestly and early; a client who hears about a problem from you, with a plan, trusts you more than one who finds it later. Where your numbers differ from the client's CRM or store data, explain why, as covered in Module 7.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":null},{"id":"scope","h":"Scope, expectations and contracts","html":"<p>Most client friction comes from unspoken expectations. Write down what is included (campaigns, reporting, calls, response times), what isn't (landing page builds, creative production, tracking work on their site), how changes to ad spend are approved, and how long results realistically take given the learning period and the sales cycle. Never promise specific results; the auction and the market are not yours to guarantee.</p><p>Cover confidentiality, data protection, notice for termination, and who owns what on exit, using the ownership table above. Start from a template that a qualified professional has reviewed.</p>","callout":{"k":"warn","t":"Contract, data protection and tax rules vary by country. This course gives general good practice, not legal advice."},"figure":null,"widget":null},{"id":"handover","h":"Offboarding and handover","html":"<p>Every client relationship ends eventually, and how it ends is part of your reputation. Because the client owns the assets, a handover is mostly documentation and removing your own access. Work through the checklist, and don't delete or alter anything on exit; the history belongs to them.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":"ag-hand"}],"takeaways":["Get access through your own manager account, never through a shared password.","The client owns the ads account, billing, analytics, tags, feed and domain; you own your manager account and your methods.","Keep ad spend and your management fee separate on paper.","Onboarding includes a tracking audit and recorded baselines before any changes.","An operating system is seven repeatable layers, not software.","Report outcomes first, misses early, and never guarantee results."],"action":"Write a one-page ownership and access policy for your own future clients: who owns each asset, which access level you will request, and how billing will work. Compare it with the table in this module.","quiz":[{"q":"Who should normally own the billing profile for a client's ads account?","opts":["The agency, always","The client","Google"],"correct":1,"explain":"Ad spend is the client's money and liability, so the billing profile should sit with them."},{"q":"Why work through your own manager account rather than a shared login?","opts":["Shared logins are not allowed on any account","Access is traceable, removable and doesn't rest on one password","It lowers CPC"],"correct":1,"explain":"A manager link can be granted and removed cleanly, which a shared password cannot offer."},{"q":"What should happen before you change anything in a newly inherited account?","opts":["Launch new campaigns at once","Audit tracking and record baselines","Send the first invoice and wait"],"correct":1,"explain":"Without trusted tracking and baselines, you cannot prove your later improvements."},{"q":"What should a client report lead with?","opts":["Outcomes tied to the agreed goals","Every metric available","Impressions only"],"correct":0,"explain":"Clients want to know whether their money is working, so outcomes come first."}],"related":[{"t":"Account Setup & Infrastructure","d":"Manager accounts and linking.","to":"m/setup"},{"t":"Glossary: Manager account (MCC)","d":"Definition and context.","to":"glossary/Manager account (MCC)"},{"t":"Tracking, Metrics & Analytics","d":"Reconciling numbers with a client's CRM.","to":"m/tracking"}]},{"id":"pro","n":11,"title":"Going Professional","sub":"Turning skill into proof, and proof into work.","intro":"Knowing Google Ads and earning a living from it are different skills. This module turns what you have learned into something other people can see and trust, helps you choose a path, and gives you a realistic plan for landing paid work.","sections":[{"id":"paths","h":"Three paths: freelance, agency and in-house","html":"<p>There are three common ways to work in paid search, and none is better in general; they reward different strengths. Freelancers run a small business of their own, agencies work in teams across many clients, and in-house marketers go deep on one company. The table compares how much each skill matters on each path.</p>","callout":{"k":"info","t":"Core means you cannot do the job without it. High means you use it constantly. Medium means it helps. Lower means it is rarely the deciding factor. These are general patterns, and every role differs."},"figure":null,"widget":"p-path"},{"id":"exp","h":"Building experience before you have a client","html":"<p>You do not need a client to start learning from real data. Build a small site or landing page of your own, offer to help a friend's or relative's local business with their permission, or practise in an account with a tiny budget you can afford to lose. Real money, even a small amount, teaches things a demo never will, such as how slow conversions arrive and how messy search terms look.</p><p>Keep honest notes as you go: what you changed, why, what you expected and what actually happened. Those notes become the raw material for case studies later. Never present a practice account as client work, and never claim results you cannot show in the account itself.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":null},{"id":"skills","h":"Mapping your own skills","html":"<p>Rate yourself honestly from 0 to 5 on the six skill areas below and watch the shape appear. A radar is not a score to maximise; it is a map of where you are strong and where the next hour of study pays off most. Most beginners are strongest on campaign mechanics and weakest on commercial skills such as pricing and selling, and that gap is normal.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":"p-radar"},{"id":"credibility","h":"Certifications and credibility","html":"<p>Google offers free training and certifications through <strong>Skillshop</strong>, including Search and measurement certifications. They show you have covered the fundamentals, and some clients and employers look for them, but they don't prove you can produce results. Treat them as a baseline.</p><p>Agencies can also apply for <strong>Google Partner</strong> status, whose requirements cover factors such as ad spend, account performance and certified staff. Requirements change, so check the current criteria rather than relying on this course. The most persuasive credential is always evidence: real accounts, real numbers, and clients willing to vouch for you.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":null},{"id":"portfolio","h":"Portfolios and case studies","html":"<p>With no clients yet, build proof from projects: your own site, a friend's business, a local charity, or a fictional business built in a sandbox and clearly labelled as such. One well-explained project is worth more than a list of tools you have used.</p><p>A good case study follows a simple arc: the situation, what you did and why, the results, and what you learned. Use the tabs below as a template. Always get permission before naming a client or showing real numbers, anonymise where needed, and never invent or inflate results.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":"p-case"},{"id":"clients","h":"Proposals, pricing and first clients","html":"<p>Price from the value you create and the scope you take on, not from an hourly guess. Offer a defined starting package, such as an audit of an existing account with prioritised recommendations, and a clearly scoped monthly management package. Keep ad spend separate from your fee in every proposal.</p><p>For first clients, start with people who already trust you: former colleagues, friends' businesses, local trades and shops. Qualify before you accept. Good clients have a real offer, a landing page that works or can be fixed, a budget that matches their goals, and someone who can make decisions. Red flags include no tracking and no willingness to add it, demands for guaranteed results, and budgets far too small for the stated target.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":null},{"id":"ethics","h":"Professional ethics and policy","html":"<p>Your reputation is your product. Report results honestly, including the disappointing ones. Don't guarantee outcomes. Follow Google Ads policies, especially in restricted categories, and handle customer data lawfully, as covered in Module 3. Don't use a competitor's trademark in ways that break policy, never try to evade a suspension, and tell clients about conflicts of interest, for example if you earn money from a tool you recommend.</p>","callout":{"k":"warn","t":"Advertising policy, trademark rules and data protection law differ by country and change over time. Check current official sources for your market."},"figure":null,"widget":null},{"id":"current","h":"Staying current","html":"<p>Google Ads changes constantly, so a one-off course is a starting point. Make reading the official Google Ads Help Centre and the product announcements a monthly habit, and keep an eye on the Google Ads Liaison's public updates and Think with Google for research on how people search and buy. Release notes tell you what actually shipped, which is more useful than commentary about what might.</p><p>Treat every new feature as a hypothesis to test on a small budget, not a default to switch on. When a source makes a claim about performance, ask what the data was and who published it. Check the current Google Ads Help Centre for the details before you teach or sell anything that may have changed.</p>","callout":null,"figure":null,"widget":null},{"id":"plan","h":"A 90-day plan and your map of the course","html":"<p>Days 1 to 30: finish the modules, build one sandbox or own-site project, and write your first case study. Days 31 to 60: offer three free or low-cost audits to people you know, document what you find, and ask for a testimonial if the work earns one. Days 61 to 90: turn the best audit into a paid package, send proposals to qualified prospects, and onboard your first client with the process from Module 10.</p><p>The map below shows the whole course on one page. Every module is a link, so use it for revision, and return to the module that matches the weakest point on your radar.</p>","callout":null,"figure":"mindmap","widget":null}],"takeaways":["Freelance, agency and in-house paths reward different mixes of the same skills.","Map your skills honestly; commercial skills are the usual gap for beginners.","Certifications are a baseline; documented results are the proof.","A case study needs the situation, the actions, the results and the lessons, with permission.","Keep ad spend out of your fee, qualify clients, and never guarantee results.","Ethics and policy compliance protect your reputation as much as your results."],"action":"Run the radar honestly, pick your lowest-scoring skill, and schedule two hours this week on the matching module. Then draft the outline of your first case study using the four tabs.","quiz":[{"q":"What best proves your ability to a prospective client?","opts":["A certification badge on its own","A documented project with honestly reported results","A long list of tools you have used"],"correct":1,"explain":"Evidence of real work and real numbers is more persuasive than badges alone."},{"q":"Which path leans most heavily on selling and pricing?","opts":["Freelance","In-house","They rely on it equally"],"correct":0,"explain":"Freelancers must find their own clients and set their own prices, so selling is a core skill."},{"q":"How should a proposal treat ad spend?","opts":["Hide it inside the fee","Keep it separate from the management fee","Leave it out of the conversation"],"correct":1,"explain":"Separating spend from fee keeps pricing honest and the scope clear."},{"q":"A prospect demands guaranteed results within two weeks. What is the professional response?","opts":["Agree to win the work","Explain realistic timelines, decline to guarantee, and walk away if they insist","Double your fee"],"correct":1,"explain":"Nobody can guarantee auction results, and the demand is a red flag."}],"related":[{"t":"Agency Operations & Client Management","d":"The onboarding and ownership process you will use.","to":"m/agency"},{"t":"Glossary: Google Partner","d":"What the badge involves.","to":"glossary/Google Partner"},{"t":"Visual Library","d":"Every diagram in the course.","to":"visuals"}]}],"glossary":[{"t":"Ad Rank","d":"The score Google calculates at auction time from your bid, expected CTR, ad relevance, landing page experience, asset impact and context. It decides whether your ad shows and where."},{"t":"Ad group","d":"A container inside a campaign that holds a theme of keywords and the ads or assets that go with them."},{"t":"AI Max for Search","d":"A set of AI-driven options for Search campaigns that widens matching beyond your keywords using your landing pages and assets. Check the current Google Ads Help Centre for exact settings."},{"t":"Asset","d":"A single building block of an ad, such as a headline, description, image, logo, video or sitelink. Google combines assets into ads."},{"t":"Bid strategy","d":"The rule that sets your bids, either manually or automatically toward a goal such as conversions or conversion value."},{"t":"Broad match","d":"The widest keyword match type. Today it uses signals such as your landing page, other keywords and Smart Bidding to guess intent, not just to match words."},{"t":"CPC","d":"Cost per click: what you actually pay each time someone clicks your ad. Cost divided by clicks."},{"t":"CTR","d":"Click-through rate: clicks divided by impressions, shown as a percentage."},{"t":"Conversion","d":"A valuable action you have told Google Ads to count, such as a purchase, a form submission or a phone call."},{"t":"Demand Gen","d":"A campaign type for visual, discovery-style placements such as YouTube and Discover that creates interest before people search."},{"t":"Exact match","d":"The tightest keyword match type. It targets searches with the same meaning as your keyword, including close variants."},{"t":"Expected CTR","d":"Google's prediction of how likely your ad is to be clicked for a keyword, compared with competitors. A component of Ad Rank."},{"t":"Impression share","d":"The share of eligible impressions your ads actually received. It shows how much demand you are missing and why."},{"t":"Landing page experience","d":"How relevant, useful and easy to use your landing page is for people who click the ad. A component of Ad Rank."},{"t":"Manager account (MCC)","d":"A top-level account used to manage many Google Ads accounts from one login, with shared reporting and access."},{"t":"Performance Max","d":"A goal-based campaign type that runs across Google's channels from one set of assets and signals, with the AI choosing placements."},{"t":"Phrase match","d":"A keyword match type that targets searches containing the meaning of your phrase, with extra words allowed before or after."},{"t":"Search term","d":"The exact words a person typed that triggered your ad. The search terms report shows them and is the best source of truth for matching."},{"t":"Smart Bidding","d":"Google's automated bid strategies that use machine learning to set bids at auction time toward a conversion goal."},{"t":"Google tag","d":"A single piece of website code that can send events to Google Ads, GA4 and other Google products at once."},{"t":"Google Tag Manager","d":"A free tag-management container that lets you add, edit and remove tags like the Google tag without a developer editing site code for every change."},{"t":"Conversion action","d":"One trackable action you have set up in Google Ads, such as a purchase or a lead form submission, with its own counting rule and value settings."},{"t":"Conversion value","d":"The monetary value assigned to a conversion, used by value-based bidding strategies such as Maximize conversion value and Target ROAS."},{"t":"Consent Mode","d":"A Google framework that adjusts how tags behave based on a visitor's cookie consent choice, and can model conversions statistically when consent is withheld."},{"t":"Enhanced conversions","d":"A feature that sends hashed first-party data, such as a hashed email address, alongside a conversion to improve measurement accuracy under privacy restrictions."},{"t":"GA4","d":"Google Analytics 4, Google's analytics platform, which can share conversion and audience data with Google Ads once the two accounts are linked."},{"t":"gclid","d":"The Google click identifier appended to your landing page URL when someone clicks an ad, used to attribute a later conversion back to that click."},{"t":"Auto-tagging","d":"An account setting, on by default, that automatically appends the gclid parameter to your URLs so conversions can be tracked."},{"t":"Merchant Center","d":"The Google platform that holds your product feed, used by Shopping campaigns and the Shopping surfaces of Performance Max."},{"t":"Primary conversion","d":"A conversion action marked to count toward automated bidding and headline reporting; Secondary conversions are tracked but not optimized toward."},{"t":"Audience segment","d":"A defined group of people Google Ads can target or observe, built from your own data, Google's data, or a custom definition you create."},{"t":"Remarketing","d":"Showing ads to people who have already interacted with your website, app or YouTube content."},{"t":"Customer Match","d":"A feature that lets you upload your own customer data, as hashed identifiers, to build an audience segment for targeting or exclusion."},{"t":"Optimized targeting","d":"A feature, available in some campaign types, that can show your ads to people beyond your chosen audience segments when they're predicted to convert. Check the current Google Ads Help Centre for where it applies."},{"t":"Negative keyword","d":"A keyword that blocks your ad from showing for searches containing it, used to filter out traffic you don't want to pay for."},{"t":"Search Partners","d":"A network of non-Google search sites and apps that can show your Search ads alongside Google's own results, when enabled on a campaign."},{"t":"Shopping campaign","d":"A campaign type that shows product listings — image, price, store name — built from a Merchant Center product feed."},{"t":"Target CPA","d":"An automated bid strategy that aims to get as many conversions as possible at or near a cost-per-conversion target you set."},{"t":"Target ROAS","d":"An automated bid strategy that aims to maximize conversion value at or near a target return on ad spend."},{"t":"Maximize conversions","d":"An automated bid strategy that spends your budget to get as many conversions as possible, with an optional CPA target."},{"t":"Quality Score","d":"A 1–10 diagnostic built from expected CTR, ad relevance and landing page experience, visible at the keyword level."},{"t":"Data-driven attribution","d":"Google Ads' default attribution model, which distributes conversion credit across the ads and clicks that contributed to it, based on patterns in your own account's data."},{"t":"View-through conversion","d":"A conversion credited to someone who saw, but didn't click, an ad — mostly relevant to Display and video."},{"t":"Responsive Search Ad","d":"An ad format built from multiple headlines and descriptions that Google assembles and tests in combination to find what performs best for a given search."},{"t":"ROAS","d":"Return on ad spend — conversion value divided by cost, usually expressed as a ratio or multiple."},{"t":"CPA","d":"Cost per acquisition (or per action) — the cost of a single conversion, found by dividing cost by conversions."},{"t":"CPM","d":"Cost per thousand impressions, a pricing and reporting metric most relevant to Display and video campaigns."},{"t":"Learning period","d":"A stretch after a new campaign or a significant change when automated bidding is gathering data and results can swing more than usual."},{"t":"Experiment","d":"A Google Ads feature that tests a change, such as a new bid strategy or budget, against a held-out share of traffic before you apply it to the whole campaign."},{"t":"Portfolio bid strategy","d":"A bid strategy shared by several campaigns that optimizes toward one combined target, such as a CPA or ROAS."},{"t":"Ad Strength","d":"A Poor-to-Excellent rating of how much variety and relevance a responsive ad's assets offer. It is a guide, not a promise of performance."},{"t":"Asset group","d":"In Performance Max, the container that holds one theme's images, logos, videos, headlines and descriptions, with its landing pages and audience signals."},{"t":"Offline conversion import","d":"Uploading conversions that happen outside your website, such as a closed sale, and linking them to the original ad click so bidding can learn from real outcomes."},{"t":"Conversion lag","d":"The delay between a click and the conversion it leads to. Recent days always look incomplete because some conversions haven't arrived yet."},{"t":"Change history","d":"A log in Google Ads of the edits made to an account, showing what changed, when, and by whom."},{"t":"Pacing","d":"How actual spend compares with the planned spend for the point you've reached in the month, used to project where you will finish."},{"t":"Auction Insights","d":"A report showing which other advertisers appear in the same auctions as you, and how you compare on impression share, overlap and position."},{"t":"Optimization score","d":"A percentage Google shows for how fully an account has applied its suggested recommendations. It is a prompt for review, not a performance metric."},{"t":"Standard access","d":"An account access level that normally lets a person work on campaigns day to day without managing account users. Check the Help Centre for exact permissions."},{"t":"Admin access","d":"The highest account access level, which can manage users as well as campaigns. Check the Help Centre for exact permissions, including billing."},{"t":"Invoiced billing","d":"A monthly invoicing arrangement for eligible advertisers, as an alternative to paying by card or direct debit. It is subject to Google's approval."},{"t":"Looker Studio","d":"Google's free reporting tool for building dashboards from sources such as Google Ads and GA4."},{"t":"Incrementality","d":"The extra conversions that happened because of your ads, rather than conversions that would have happened anyway."},{"t":"Marginal CPA","d":"The cost of the next additional conversion you buy. It is usually higher than your average CPA as spend grows."},{"t":"Break-even ROAS","d":"The return on ad spend at which a sale just covers its costs: 1 divided by gross margin. At a 40% margin, that is 2.5x."},{"t":"Tag Assistant","d":"A Google tool that shows which tags fire on a page, used to verify that tracking works."},{"t":"Skillshop","d":"Google's free training platform, where Google Ads certifications are taken."},{"t":"Google Partner","d":"A badge for agencies and professionals who meet Google's requirements, which cover factors such as spend, performance and certification. Check the current criteria."}],"qa":[{"q":"I switched to Broad match with Smart Bidding and my search terms look messy. Did I break something?","a":"Not necessarily. Broad match now leans on signals such as your landing page, your other keywords and conversion history, so the search terms report will look looser than it did under the old keyword-era rules. Judge it by outcomes, not by how tidy the terms look. Check three things: whether conversion tracking is accurate, whether CPA or ROAS is inside your target over a full cycle, and whether clearly irrelevant themes keep appearing. If the third is true, add negative keywords at the right level and tighten the landing page message. If the first is shaky, fix tracking before anything else, because Smart Bidding will learn from bad data faster than good. Run the change as an experiment where you can, so you compare against a held-out share of traffic rather than against last month."},{"q":"When should I choose Performance Max over Search plus Shopping?","a":"Choose by what you can feed and measure, not by novelty. Search plus Shopping gives you more visibility into queries and more levers, so it is a good default when you have the time to manage it and the budget is modest. Performance Max suits accounts that already have reliable conversion data, a healthy product feed or strong assets, and enough volume for automation to learn from. It trades transparency for reach across Google's channels. A sensible path is to prove demand with Search first, then test Performance Max alongside it, using an experiment or a clear before-and-after window, and keep brand terms protected in their own Search campaign. Reporting detail and available controls change often, so check the current Google Ads Help Centre before promising a client a specific breakdown."},{"q":"How do I stop brand searches from flattering my Performance Max results?","a":"Start by admitting the problem: if brand searches are cheap, high-converting and easy to win, any campaign that captures them will look excellent. Keep a dedicated brand Search campaign so you can see brand performance on its own. Then check how your Performance Max campaign is behaving, using the reporting available to you, and compare its non-brand contribution with its total. Google has offered brand-related controls for Performance Max, but availability and exact behaviour change, so check the current Google Ads Help Centre rather than assuming. Also ask the incrementality question: would these customers have found the business anyway? A brand-heavy campaign can hide weak prospecting. Report brand and non-brand separately to the client so the numbers mean what they appear to mean."},{"q":"Google Ads shows 20 conversions and GA4 shows 14. Which one do I trust?","a":"Neither blindly. The two systems count differently: attribution models, conversion windows, consent handling, cross-device matching and the moment a conversion is credited can all differ, so a gap is normal. What matters is whether the gap is stable and explainable. Pick a source of truth for the business outcome, usually the CRM or the shop's own order records, and reconcile both platforms against it. Then check the basics: are the same events counted, is one counting every conversion while the other counts one per session, is a tag firing twice, and did consent settings change. Use Google Ads numbers for bidding, because that is what the algorithm learns from, and use the business records for judging real results. Write the reconciliation rule down so the client sees one consistent story."},{"q":"How long should I wait before judging a change?","a":"Long enough for the data to be meaningful, which depends on volume more than on the calendar. After a significant edit such as a new bid strategy, a large budget move or a big targeting change, automated bidding can need a while to settle, and results often swing in the meantime. A practical approach is to wait at least a full business cycle, for example one to two weeks for fast-moving accounts and longer for slow lead cycles, and to remember conversion lag: recent days always look incomplete. Make one change at a time so you know what caused what, log it, and compare like with like. If numbers are alarming, check tracking and policy status first, then decide. Reacting daily to noise is a common way to keep a campaign permanently in learning."},{"q":"Should I consolidate campaigns or keep them segmented?","a":"It depends on data volume and on whether the segments genuinely need different goals. Automated bidding learns better with more conversions in one place, so very small campaigns that each get a handful of conversions often benefit from consolidation. But merge only things that share the same economics. Brand and non-brand, products with very different margins, or markets with different targets should usually stay apart, because one blended target will starve one and overspend on the other. A good test is whether you would set a different CPA or ROAS target for each. If yes, separate them. If no, try combining and watch carefully. Also remember budgets and reporting: segments make clean reporting easier, consolidation makes learning easier. Pick the one the account actually needs, and revisit it as volume grows."},{"q":"The client wants a lower CPA and more volume at the same time. What do I say?","a":"Explain the trade-off with marginal CPA. The average CPA in the report blends cheap early conversions with the expensive ones you buy as spend grows, so each extra increment usually costs more than the average suggests. Ask what a conversion is actually worth: if a lead is worth far more than its cost, accepting a higher CPA for more volume may be profitable. Work out break-even from margin or lead value, then agree a range rather than a single number. Offer options: hold the target and accept slower growth, loosen it to buy more volume, or improve the landing page and offer to move both numbers together. Put the agreed decision in writing so the next review compares results against a shared rule, not against hope."},{"q":"I have inherited a messy account. What do I do first?","a":"Resist the urge to rebuild immediately. First secure access properly through your manager account, then audit before touching anything: confirm conversion tracking works, check which conversions are Primary, review linked accounts, billing status, policy notices, change history and the search terms. Record baselines for spend, conversions, CPA or ROAS and impression share, so you can show what changed and why. Fix tracking and obvious waste first, such as irrelevant search terms and wrong location settings, because those improve results without restarting much learning. Then make structural changes one at a time, ideally as experiments, and log each in a running change note. Tell the client what you found in plain language, and agree a 30-day plan with owners and dates before large edits begin."}],"templates":[{"t":"Client Onboarding Checklist","b":"CLIENT ONBOARDING CHECKLIST\nClient: [business name]    Date: [YYYY-MM-DD]    Owner: [your name]\n\n1. Discovery\n[ ] What the business sells and to whom: [notes]\n[ ] Gross margin: [__]%    Average order or lead value: [__]\n[ ] Break-even CPA or ROAS: [__]\n[ ] Sales cycle and lead follow-up process: [notes]\n[ ] Success in the client's own words: [notes]\n\n2. Access (lowest level that works)\n[ ] Manager account link request sent to: [client email]\n[ ] GA4 role: [__]    Tag Manager role: [__]    Merchant Center role: [__]\n[ ] Access recorded in the access log on: [date]\n\n3. Audit before changing anything\n[ ] Primary conversions: [list]; test conversion recorded on [date]\n[ ] Auto-tagging, linked accounts, currency and time zone confirmed\n[ ] Search terms, policy status and landing pages reviewed\n\n4. Baselines (last [__] days)\nSpend: [__]   Conversions: [__]   CPA or ROAS: [__]   Impression share: [__]\n\n5. Agreements\n[ ] KPIs and targets agreed in writing: [list]\n[ ] Report day and format: [__]    Call cadence: [__]\n[ ] Who approves budget changes: [name]\n[ ] 30-day plan sent on: [date]"},{"t":"Asset Ownership Note","b":"ASSET OWNERSHIP NOTE\nBetween: [client business name] (\"the client\") and [your name or agency] (\"the manager\")\nDate: [YYYY-MM-DD]\n\nThe client owns the following and will keep them if this engagement ends:\n- Google Ads account ID: [__-__-____]\n- Billing profile and payment method: [client name]\n- Google Analytics 4 property: [property name / ID]\n- Tag Manager container: [container ID]\n- Merchant Center account (if used): [ID]\n- Website domain and hosting: [domain]\n\nThe manager will:\n- Work through the manager account [ID] at [Standard / Admin] access\n- Never ask for or share passwords\n- Keep a change log and share it on request: [frequency]\n\nAd spend is paid by the client directly to Google. The management fee of [amount] per [month] is invoiced separately.\n\nOn ending: the manager removes access within [__] days of written notice and hands over the change log and documentation.\n\nSigned (client): [name, date]\nSigned (manager): [name, date]\n\nThis note is a plain-language summary, not legal advice. Have a qualified professional review your contract."},{"t":"Pre-Launch Checklist","b":"PRE-LAUNCH CHECKLIST\nCampaign: [name]    Client: [name]    Launch date: [YYYY-MM-DD]\n\nTracking\n[ ] Conversion action(s) set up: [list]; Primary: [list]\n[ ] A real test conversion recorded and visible on [date]\n[ ] Auto-tagging on; Google tag fires on [page URLs]\n[ ] GA4 linked: [yes / no]\n\nAccount\n[ ] Currency [__] and time zone [__] correct\n[ ] Billing active: [payment method confirmed by client]\n\nCampaign\n[ ] Goal and campaign type match: [goal] / [type]\n[ ] Budget: [__] per day    Bid strategy: [__]\n[ ] Locations: [list]; presence option checked\n[ ] Languages, schedule and networks checked\n[ ] Negative keywords added: [list]\n\nAds and pages\n[ ] Headlines and descriptions are distinct, accurate and within policy\n[ ] Final URLs load fast on mobile and match the ad message\n[ ] Privacy policy page and consent banner live\n\nApproval\n[ ] Client approved on: [date]    Reviewed by: [name]"},{"t":"Ad Copy / Creative Test Brief","b":"AD COPY / CREATIVE TEST BRIEF\nClient: [name]    Campaign / ad group: [name]    Date: [YYYY-MM-DD]\n\nHypothesis\nIf we change [one variable], then [metric] will [rise / fall] because [reason].\n\nTest design\nVariable being changed: [headline / image / offer / call to action]\nControl (A): [current version]\nVariant (B): [new version]\nEverything else held constant: [targeting, budget split, landing page]\n\nAudience and offer\nWho it is for: [audience]    Main benefit: [__]    Proof point: [__]\nWords and claims to avoid (policy, legal, brand): [list]\n\nMeasurement\nPrimary metric: [conversion rate / CPA / CTR]\nMinimum run time: [__] days    Minimum clicks per variant: [__]\nDecision rule: keep B only if the gap is clearly larger than normal variation.\n\nResult (fill in after the test)\nA: [clicks] clicks, [conversions] conversions\nB: [clicks] clicks, [conversions] conversions\nDecision: [keep A / keep B / keep testing]    Next test: [__]"},{"t":"Weekly Client Update","b":"WEEKLY CLIENT UPDATE\nTo: [client name]    Week ending: [YYYY-MM-DD]    From: [your name]\n\nHeadline\n[One sentence on whether the money is working, in plain language.]\n\nThe numbers (vs [last week / target])\nSpend: [__]    Conversions: [__]    CPA or ROAS: [__]\nPacing against the monthly budget: [on track / high / low]\n\nWhat happened\n- [Result or change 1 and why it matters]\n- [Result or change 2 and why it matters]\n\nWhat I did this week\n- [Action 1]\n- [Action 2]\n\nWhat I plan next week\n- [Planned test or fix, with the reason]\n\nWhat I need from you\n- [Decision, asset or access, with a date]\n\nWatch-outs\n- [Risk, delay, tracking note or seasonality]\n\nNext call: [date and time]"},{"t":"Naming Convention","b":"Campaign name: [Client]_[Type]_[Goal]_[Geo]_[Date]\nExample: Acme_Search_Leads_UK_2026-10\n\nAd group name: [Theme]_[MatchStrategy]\nExample: EmergencyPlumber_Broad\n\nRules: no spaces, same order every time, date as YYYY-MM."}],"figures":{"structure":{"t":"Account structure","cap":"Each level controls something different: access, billing, goals, themes, and what people see.","svg":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 520 360\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\"><title id=\"f1t\">Google Ads account structure</title><desc id=\"f1d\">Manager account, ad account, campaign, ad group, then ads and assets.</desc>\n<rect x=\"110\" y=\"8\" width=\"300\" height=\"52\" rx=\"10\" style=\"fill:var(--g-blue-50);stroke:var(--g-blue);stroke-width:2\"/><text x=\"260\" y=\"31\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"500\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">Manager account</text><text x=\"260\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"12\"  text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">Access and reporting across accounts</text><rect x=\"110\" y=\"78\" width=\"300\" height=\"52\" rx=\"10\" style=\"fill:var(--g-red-50);stroke:var(--g-red);stroke-width:2\"/><text x=\"260\" y=\"101\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"500\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">Ad account</text><text x=\"260\" y=\"119\" font-size=\"12\"  text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">Billing, currency, time zone</text><rect x=\"110\" y=\"148\" width=\"300\" height=\"52\" rx=\"10\" style=\"fill:var(--g-yellow);stroke:var(--g-yellow);stroke-width:2\"/><text x=\"260\" y=\"171\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"500\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--on-y);font-family:var(--hd)\">Campaign</text><text x=\"260\" y=\"189\" font-size=\"12\"  text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--on-y);font-family:var(--hd)\">Type, budget, bidding, locations</text><rect x=\"110\" y=\"218\" width=\"300\" height=\"52\" rx=\"10\" style=\"fill:var(--g-green-50);stroke:var(--g-green);stroke-width:2\"/><text x=\"260\" y=\"241\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"500\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">Ad group</text><text x=\"260\" y=\"259\" font-size=\"12\"  text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">One theme of keywords</text><rect x=\"110\" y=\"288\" width=\"300\" height=\"52\" rx=\"10\" style=\"fill:var(--surface);stroke:var(--line);stroke-width:2\"/><text x=\"260\" y=\"311\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"500\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">Ads and assets</text><text x=\"260\" y=\"329\" font-size=\"12\"  text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">Headlines, images, keywords</text>\n<path d=\"M260 60v18M260 130v18M260 200v18M260 270v18\" stroke-width=\"2\" style=\"stroke:var(--ink-soft)\"/></svg>"},"adrank":{"t":"How Ad Rank works","cap":"A teaching view. Google's real auction has many more signals.","svg":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 560 250\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f3t f3d\"><title id=\"f3t\">Ad Rank diagram</title><desc id=\"f3d\">Bid and quality signals, asset impact and context combine into Ad Rank, which sets position. You pay just enough to beat the next ad.</desc>\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"10\" width=\"150\" height=\"44\" rx=\"10\" style=\"fill:var(--g-blue-50);stroke:var(--g-blue);stroke-width:2\"/><text x=\"85\" y=\"37\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"500\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">Your bid</text><rect x=\"10\" y=\"64\" width=\"150\" height=\"88\" rx=\"10\" style=\"fill:var(--g-red-50);stroke:var(--g-red);stroke-width:2\"/><text x=\"85\" y=\"105\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"500\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">Ad quality</text><text x=\"85\" y=\"123\" font-size=\"12\"  text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">Expected CTR · Relevance · Landing page</text><rect x=\"10\" y=\"162\" width=\"150\" height=\"36\" rx=\"10\" style=\"fill:var(--g-yellow);stroke:var(--g-yellow);stroke-width:2\"/><text x=\"85\" y=\"185\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"500\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--on-y);font-family:var(--hd)\">Asset impact</text><rect x=\"10\" y=\"206\" width=\"150\" height=\"36\" rx=\"10\" style=\"fill:var(--g-green-50);stroke:var(--g-green);stroke-width:2\"/><text x=\"85\" y=\"229\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"500\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">Context</text>\n<path d=\"M160 32h40v90M160 108h40M160 180h40v-58M160 224h40V122\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\" style=\"stroke:var(--ink-soft)\"/><rect x=\"200\" y=\"92\" width=\"130\" height=\"60\" rx=\"10\" style=\"fill:var(--g-blue-50);stroke:var(--g-blue);stroke-width:2\"/><text x=\"265\" y=\"127\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"500\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">Ad Rank</text><path d=\"M330 122h40\" stroke-width=\"2\" style=\"stroke:var(--ink-soft)\"/><rect x=\"370\" y=\"92\" width=\"110\" height=\"60\" rx=\"10\" style=\"fill:var(--surface);stroke:var(--line);stroke-width:2\"/><text x=\"425\" y=\"127\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"500\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">Position</text><path d=\"M425 152v34\" stroke-width=\"2\" style=\"stroke:var(--ink-soft)\"/><text x=\"480\" y=\"206\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"500\" text-anchor=\"end\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">You pay just enough</text><text x=\"480\" y=\"224\" font-size=\"13\"  text-anchor=\"end\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">to beat the next ad</text></svg>"},"match":{"t":"Keyword match types","cap":"Illustrative queries for the keyword “running shoes”. Matching is looser than it looks.","svg":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 520 280\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f4t f4d\"><title id=\"f4t\">Match types</title><desc id=\"f4d\">Broad contains phrase, which contains exact.</desc>\n<rect x=\"8\" y=\"8\" width=\"504\" height=\"264\" rx=\"24\" style=\"fill:var(--g-blue-50);stroke:var(--g-blue);stroke-width:2\"/><text x=\"28\" y=\"38\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"500\" text-anchor=\"start\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">Broad: related meaning, e.g. “best trainers for jogging”</text>\n<rect x=\"40\" y=\"62\" width=\"440\" height=\"190\" rx=\"20\" style=\"fill:var(--g-green-50);stroke:var(--g-green);stroke-width:2\"/><text x=\"60\" y=\"92\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"500\" text-anchor=\"start\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">Phrase: same meaning, e.g. “buy running shoes online”</text>\n<rect x=\"84\" y=\"116\" width=\"352\" height=\"112\" rx=\"16\" style=\"fill:var(--bg);stroke:var(--g-red);stroke-width:2\"/><text x=\"260\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"500\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">Exact: “running shoes”</text><text x=\"260\" y=\"190\" font-size=\"12\"  text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">and close variants of that meaning</text></svg>"},"serp":{"t":"Search results anatomy","cap":"A simplified mock. Layout and labels change over time.","svg":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 520 330\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f7t f7d\"><title id=\"f7t\">Search results anatomy</title><desc id=\"f7d\">A search bar, a sponsored result with headline, display URL, description, sitelinks and callouts, and one organic result.</desc>\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"8\" width=\"340\" height=\"36\" rx=\"18\" style=\"fill:var(--surface);stroke:var(--line);stroke-width:2\"/><rect x=\"30\" y=\"22\" width=\"130\" height=\"8\" rx=\"4\" style=\"fill:var(--line)\"/>\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"60\" width=\"340\" height=\"170\" rx=\"12\" style=\"fill:var(--bg);stroke:var(--g-blue);stroke-width:2\"/><text x=\"22\" y=\"82\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"500\" text-anchor=\"start\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">Sponsored</text><text x=\"22\" y=\"106\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"500\" text-anchor=\"start\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">Fast emergency plumber near you</text><text x=\"22\" y=\"126\" font-size=\"12\"  text-anchor=\"start\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">example.com/plumber</text>\n<rect x=\"22\" y=\"138\" width=\"300\" height=\"7\" rx=\"3\" style=\"fill:var(--line)\"/><rect x=\"22\" y=\"152\" width=\"240\" height=\"7\" rx=\"3\" style=\"fill:var(--line)\"/>\n<rect x=\"22\" y=\"170\" width=\"90\" height=\"22\" rx=\"11\" style=\"fill:var(--g-blue-50)\"/><rect x=\"120\" y=\"170\" width=\"90\" height=\"22\" rx=\"11\" style=\"fill:var(--g-blue-50)\"/><rect x=\"218\" y=\"170\" width=\"90\" height=\"22\" rx=\"11\" style=\"fill:var(--g-blue-50)\"/><text x=\"22\" y=\"214\" font-size=\"12\"  text-anchor=\"start\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">✓ 24/7  ✓ Free quote  ✓ Licensed</text>\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"246\" width=\"340\" height=\"70\" rx=\"12\" style=\"fill:var(--bg);stroke:var(--line);stroke-width:2\"/><rect x=\"22\" y=\"262\" width=\"200\" height=\"9\" rx=\"4\" style=\"fill:var(--ink-soft)\"/><rect x=\"22\" y=\"280\" width=\"300\" height=\"7\" rx=\"3\" style=\"fill:var(--line)\"/><rect x=\"22\" y=\"294\" width=\"260\" height=\"7\" rx=\"3\" style=\"fill:var(--line)\"/>\n<path d=\"M350 106h20M350 126h20M350 146h20M350 181h20M350 214h20\" stroke-width=\"2\" style=\"stroke:var(--ink-soft)\"/><text x=\"374\" y=\"110\" font-size=\"13\"  text-anchor=\"start\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">Headline</text><text x=\"374\" y=\"130\" font-size=\"13\"  text-anchor=\"start\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">Display URL</text><text x=\"374\" y=\"150\" font-size=\"13\"  text-anchor=\"start\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">Description</text><text x=\"374\" y=\"185\" font-size=\"13\"  text-anchor=\"start\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">Sitelinks</text><text x=\"374\" y=\"218\" font-size=\"13\"  text-anchor=\"start\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">Callouts</text></svg>"},"funnel":{"t":"Campaign-type funnel","cap":"Match the type to where the friction is.","svg":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 520 300\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\"><title id=\"f2t\">Campaign-type funnel</title><desc id=\"f2d\">Awareness campaign types narrow down to consideration, then to conversion-focused types.</desc>\n<rect x=\"30\" y=\"10\" width=\"460\" height=\"70\" rx=\"10\" style=\"fill:var(--g-blue-50);stroke:var(--g-blue);stroke-width:2\"/><text x=\"260\" y=\"42\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"500\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">Awareness</text><text x=\"260\" y=\"60\" font-size=\"12\"  text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">YouTube · Demand Gen · Display</text>\n<rect x=\"80\" y=\"110\" width=\"360\" height=\"70\" rx=\"10\" style=\"fill:var(--g-yellow);stroke:var(--g-yellow);stroke-width:2\"/><text x=\"260\" y=\"142\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"500\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--on-y);font-family:var(--hd)\">Consideration</text><text x=\"260\" y=\"160\" font-size=\"12\"  text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--on-y);font-family:var(--hd)\">Demand Gen · Video · Search (non-brand)</text>\n<rect x=\"130\" y=\"210\" width=\"260\" height=\"70\" rx=\"10\" style=\"fill:var(--g-green-50);stroke:var(--g-green);stroke-width:2\"/><text x=\"260\" y=\"242\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"500\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">Conversion</text><text x=\"260\" y=\"260\" font-size=\"12\"  text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">Search · Shopping · Performance Max</text>\n<path d=\"M260 80v20M260 180v20\" stroke-width=\"2\" style=\"stroke:var(--ink-soft)\"/></svg>"},"flow":{"t":"Conversion data flow","cap":"How one on-site action becomes a bidding signal.","svg":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 520 420\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f5t f5d\"><title id=\"f5t\">Conversion data flow</title><desc id=\"f5d\">A visitor's action travels through the website, the Google tag, enhanced conversions and consent mode, to Google Ads, and finally to Smart Bidding.</desc>\n<rect x=\"60\" y=\"8\" width=\"400\" height=\"50\" rx=\"10\" style=\"fill:var(--g-blue-50);stroke:var(--g-blue);stroke-width:2\"/><text x=\"260\" y=\"38\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"500\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">Visitor</text><rect x=\"60\" y=\"76\" width=\"400\" height=\"50\" rx=\"10\" style=\"fill:var(--g-red-50);stroke:var(--g-red);stroke-width:2\"/><text x=\"260\" y=\"106\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"500\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">Website</text><rect x=\"60\" y=\"144\" width=\"400\" height=\"50\" rx=\"10\" style=\"fill:var(--g-yellow);stroke:var(--g-yellow);stroke-width:2\"/><text x=\"260\" y=\"174\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"500\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--on-y);font-family:var(--hd)\">Google tag / GTM fires</text><rect x=\"60\" y=\"212\" width=\"400\" height=\"50\" rx=\"10\" style=\"fill:var(--g-green-50);stroke:var(--g-green);stroke-width:2\"/><text x=\"260\" y=\"242\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"500\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">Enhanced conversions + Consent Mode</text><rect x=\"60\" y=\"280\" width=\"400\" height=\"50\" rx=\"10\" style=\"fill:var(--surface);stroke:var(--line);stroke-width:2\"/><text x=\"260\" y=\"310\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"500\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">Google Ads records the conversion</text><rect x=\"60\" y=\"348\" width=\"400\" height=\"50\" rx=\"10\" style=\"fill:var(--g-blue-50);stroke:var(--g-blue);stroke-width:2\"/><text x=\"260\" y=\"378\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"500\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">Smart Bidding uses it</text>\n<path d=\"M260 58v18M260 126v18M260 194v18M260 262v18M260 330v18\" stroke-width=\"2\" style=\"stroke:var(--ink-soft)\"/></svg>"},"chain":{"t":"The metric chain","cap":"Illustrative numbers — the relationship matters more than the exact figures.","svg":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 520 280\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f6t f6d\"><title id=\"f6t\">The metric chain</title><desc id=\"f6d\">Impressions narrow to clicks through CTR, then to conversions through conversion rate, which together produce CPA and ROAS. Numbers are illustrative.</desc>\n<rect x=\"20\" y=\"20\" width=\"480\" height=\"40\" rx=\"8\" style=\"fill:var(--g-blue-50);stroke:var(--g-blue);stroke-width:2\"/><text x=\"32\" y=\"45\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"500\" text-anchor=\"start\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">Impressions — 50,000</text>\n<text x=\"20\" y=\"80\" font-size=\"12\"  text-anchor=\"start\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">× CTR 4%</text>\n<rect x=\"20\" y=\"96\" width=\"160\" height=\"40\" rx=\"8\" style=\"fill:var(--g-red-50);stroke:var(--g-red);stroke-width:2\"/><text x=\"32\" y=\"121\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"500\" text-anchor=\"start\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">Clicks — 2,000</text>\n<text x=\"20\" y=\"156\" font-size=\"12\"  text-anchor=\"start\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">× CVR 5%</text>\n<rect x=\"20\" y=\"172\" width=\"60\" height=\"40\" rx=\"8\" style=\"fill:var(--g-green-50);stroke:var(--g-green);stroke-width:2\"/><text x=\"90\" y=\"197\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"500\" text-anchor=\"start\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">Conversions — 100</text>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"96\" width=\"180\" height=\"40\" rx=\"8\" style=\"fill:var(--surface);stroke:var(--line);stroke-width:2\"/><text x=\"332\" y=\"121\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"500\" text-anchor=\"start\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">CPA = cost ÷ 100</text>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"172\" width=\"180\" height=\"40\" rx=\"8\" style=\"fill:var(--surface);stroke:var(--line);stroke-width:2\"/><text x=\"332\" y=\"197\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"500\" text-anchor=\"start\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">ROAS = value ÷ cost</text>\n<text x=\"20\" y=\"260\" font-size=\"12\"  text-anchor=\"start\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">Illustrative numbers — the relationship, not the exact figures, is what matters.</text></svg>"},"learning":{"t":"The learning period","cap":"Cost per result swings after a change, settles as data accumulates, then swings again after the next edit.","svg":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 520 260\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f-learn-t f-learn-d\"><title id=\"f-learn-t\">The learning period</title><desc id=\"f-learn-d\">Cost per result swings erratically right after a change, settles as data accumulates, then swings again after the next significant edit.</desc>\n<line x1=\"40\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"40\" y2=\"220\" stroke-width=\"2\" style=\"stroke:var(--line)\"/><line x1=\"40\" y1=\"220\" x2=\"500\" y2=\"220\" stroke-width=\"2\" style=\"stroke:var(--line)\"/>\n<polyline points=\"40,180 55,90 70,200 85,110 100,170 115,100 130,150 160,130 190,120 220,115 250,112 280,110\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" style=\"stroke:var(--g-blue-600)\"/>\n<line x1=\"280\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"280\" y2=\"220\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 4\" style=\"stroke:var(--g-red)\"/>\n<polyline points=\"280,110 295,170 310,95 325,160 340,115 370,118 400,113 430,112 460,111\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" style=\"stroke:var(--g-green-600)\"/>\n<text x=\"285\" y=\"34\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"500\" text-anchor=\"start\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">Edit made here</text>\n<text x=\"40\" y=\"240\" font-size=\"12\"  text-anchor=\"start\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">Days since change →</text>\n<text x=\"40\" y=\"255\" font-size=\"12\"  text-anchor=\"start\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">Illustrative shape, not real data.</text></svg>"},"cmind":{"t":"The creative system, mapped","cap":"How the pieces of an ad relate to each other.","svg":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 600 320\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f-cmind-t f-cmind-d\"><title id=\"f-cmind-t\">The creative system, mapped</title><desc id=\"f-cmind-d\">Six parts of the creative system radiate from a central creative hub: RSAs, assets, Performance Max asset groups, Demand Gen creative, testing discipline and policy.</desc>\n<line x1=\"300\" y1=\"160\" x2=\"300\" y2=\"40\" stroke-width=\"2\" style=\"stroke:var(--line)\"/><line x1=\"300\" y1=\"160\" x2=\"460\" y2=\"90\" stroke-width=\"2\" style=\"stroke:var(--line)\"/><line x1=\"300\" y1=\"160\" x2=\"460\" y2=\"230\" stroke-width=\"2\" style=\"stroke:var(--line)\"/><line x1=\"300\" y1=\"160\" x2=\"300\" y2=\"280\" stroke-width=\"2\" style=\"stroke:var(--line)\"/><line x1=\"300\" y1=\"160\" x2=\"140\" y2=\"230\" stroke-width=\"2\" style=\"stroke:var(--line)\"/><line x1=\"300\" y1=\"160\" x2=\"140\" y2=\"90\" stroke-width=\"2\" style=\"stroke:var(--line)\"/>\n<circle cx=\"300\" cy=\"160\" r=\"46\" style=\"fill:var(--g-blue-600)\"/><text x=\"300\" y=\"165\" font-size=\"14\"  text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--on-p);font-family:var(--hd)\">Creative</text>\n<circle cx=\"300\" cy=\"40\" r=\"40\" style=\"fill:var(--g-blue-50);stroke:var(--g-blue);stroke-width:2\"/><text x=\"300\" y=\"45\" font-size=\"12.5\"  text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">RSA</text>\n<circle cx=\"460\" cy=\"90\" r=\"46\" style=\"fill:var(--g-red-50);stroke:var(--g-red);stroke-width:2\"/><text x=\"460\" y=\"88\" font-size=\"12\"  text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">Assets &amp;</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"102\" font-size=\"12\"  text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">extensions</text>\n<circle cx=\"460\" cy=\"230\" r=\"46\" style=\"fill:var(--g-yellow);stroke:var(--g-yellow);stroke-width:2\"/><text x=\"460\" y=\"228\" font-size=\"12\"  text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--on-y);font-family:var(--hd)\">PMax asset</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"242\" font-size=\"12\"  text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--on-y);font-family:var(--hd)\">groups</text>\n<circle cx=\"300\" cy=\"280\" r=\"40\" style=\"fill:var(--g-green-50);stroke:var(--g-green);stroke-width:2\"/><text x=\"300\" y=\"278\" font-size=\"12\"  text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">Demand Gen</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"292\" font-size=\"12\"  text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">creative</text>\n<circle cx=\"140\" cy=\"230\" r=\"46\" style=\"fill:var(--g-blue-50);stroke:var(--g-blue);stroke-width:2\"/><text x=\"140\" y=\"228\" font-size=\"12\"  text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">Testing</text><text x=\"140\" y=\"242\" font-size=\"12\"  text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">discipline</text>\n<circle cx=\"140\" cy=\"90\" r=\"40\" style=\"fill:var(--g-red-50);stroke:var(--g-red);stroke-width:2\"/><text x=\"140\" y=\"95\" font-size=\"14\"  text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)\">Policy</text></svg>"},"mindmap":{"t":"The whole course, mapped","cap":"Eleven modules around one hub. Select any module to open it.","svg":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 600 400\" role=\"group\" aria-labelledby=\"f-mm-t f-mm-d\"><title id=\"f-mm-t\">The whole course, mapped</title><desc id=\"f-mm-d\">Eleven modules arranged around a central hub. Each module is a link that opens it.</desc><circle cx=\"300\" cy=\"200\" r=\"48\" style=\"fill:var(--g-blue-600)\"/><text x=\"300\" y=\"198\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" style=\"fill:var(--bg);font-family:var(--hd)\">Google Ads</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"216\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" style=\"fill:var(--bg);font-family:var(--hd)\">the course</text></svg>"}}};
const COLS = ["blue","red","yellow","green"]; /* palette used by widget simulators */
const GWIDGETS = {"h-map":{"type":"map","title":"Course map: click a module","center":"Course","link":true,"nodes":[]},"f-place":{"type":"map","title":"Where Google ads appear","center":"Your ad","nodes":[{"t":"Search","d":"Text ads on search results. You reach people who are actively looking, so intent is highest here."},{"t":"Shopping","d":"Product listings with image, price and store name, built from a Merchant Center product feed."},{"t":"YouTube","d":"Video ads in and around videos. Best for creating interest rather than capturing it."},{"t":"Display","d":"Image ads on partner websites and apps. Wide reach, usually lower intent."},{"t":"Maps / Discover","d":"Reached through Performance Max and Demand Gen, not chosen one by one. Gmail, Discover and Maps are among these surfaces."}]},"f-auction":{"type":"calc","title":"Ad Rank simulator (a teaching simplification)","fields":[{"k":"bid","l":"Your max bid ($)","min":0.5,"max":8,"step":0.1,"v":2.5},{"k":"ctr","l":"Expected CTR","o":[[1,"Below average"],[2,"Average"],[3,"Above average"]],"v":2},{"k":"rel","l":"Ad relevance","o":[[1,"Below average"],[2,"Average"],[3,"Above average"]],"v":2},{"k":"lp","l":"Landing page","o":[[1,"Below average"],[2,"Average"],[3,"Above average"]],"v":2}],"run":function(v){const b=Math.min(8,Math.max(.5,+v.bid||.5)),q=((+v.ctr||1)+(+v.rel||1)+(+v.lp||1))/3;
const rows=[{n:"You",b,q},{n:"Competitor A",b:3,q:2},{n:"Competitor B",b:2,q:2.7}].map(r=>({...r,r:r.b*r.q})).sort((a,c)=>c.r-a.r);
const w=rows[0],cpc=Math.min(w.b,rows[1].r/w.q+.01),mx=w.r||1;
return `<div class="tiles"><div class="tile"><b>${w.n}</b><span>wins the top spot</span></div><div class="tile"><b>$${cpc.toFixed(2)}</b><span>est. CPC for the winner, not its max bid</span></div></div>`+
rows.map(r=>`<div class="bar ${r===w?'w':''}"><span>${r.n}</span><i style="width:${Math.max(4,r.r/mx*60)}%"></i><span>${r.r.toFixed(1)}</span></div>`).join("")+`<p style="font-size:.875rem;color:var(--ink-soft);margin:8px 0 0">Rank = bid × average quality. Real Ad Rank uses more signals, so treat this as a model of the idea.</p>`}},"f-ai":{"type":"tabs","title":"Keyword era vs AI-first era","tabs":[{"t":"Keyword era","p":"You chose exact keywords, set bids by hand and wrote one ad per theme. Control came from the keyword list, and the search terms you saw mostly matched it."},{"t":"AI-first era","p":"You supply goals, conversion data, assets and landing pages. Broad match with Smart Bidding, AI Max for Search and Performance Max predict intent from signals. Your levers are data quality, assets and exclusions."}]},"s-portfolio":{"type":"map","title":"What lives inside your Google Ads setup","center":"Your setup","nodes":[{"t":"Manager account","d":"Holds no budget itself. It's the login that gives you access to one or more real Ads accounts, with combined reporting."},{"t":"Ads account","d":"Where campaigns, budgets and billing actually live. Currency and time zone are set once and cannot change."},{"t":"GA4 property","d":"Analytics data you can import into Google Ads as conversions or audiences once the two are linked."},{"t":"Tag Manager container","d":"Holds the Google tag and other tags, so most changes don't need a developer touching site code."},{"t":"Merchant Center","d":"Your product feed. Required for Shopping campaigns and for ecommerce Performance Max — without it, those types have nothing to sell."},{"t":"Business Profile","d":"Holds your business address, phone number and hours, which can feed location and call assets across Search and Maps."}]},"s-data":{"type":"steps","title":"How conversion data travels","steps":[{"t":"Visitor acts","d":"Someone fills a form, calls, or buys on your site."},{"t":"Google tag / GTM fires","d":"The tag sends the event, carrying the gclid from their ad click if there was one."},{"t":"Enhanced conversions hash the details","d":"If enabled, first-party details like an email address are hashed in the browser and sent alongside the event, improving matching when cookies are limited."},{"t":"Consent signal is checked","d":"Consent Mode adjusts what is sent based on the visitor's cookie choice, and can model the gap rather than losing the data outright."},{"t":"Google Ads records it","d":"The conversion is attributed back to the click or view that drove it."},{"t":"Smart Bidding learns from it","d":"Every recorded conversion becomes training signal for future bids."}]},"s-events":{"type":"steps","title":"Conversion events along the funnel","steps":[{"t":"Add to cart","d":"An early-funnel ecommerce signal. Useful for remarketing and as a secondary conversion, rarely what you optimize toward."},{"t":"Begin checkout","d":"A stronger intent signal than add-to-cart, still usually kept as secondary."},{"t":"Lead form submit","d":"The core conversion for most service businesses — usually set as Primary."},{"t":"Phone call","d":"Tracked via a call asset or a dynamic number on-site, set up separately from form tracking."},{"t":"Purchase","d":"The standard ecommerce conversion, carrying a conversion value for ROAS-based bidding."},{"t":"Sign-up","d":"A useful proxy when the real sale happens later, offline, outside what the website can see."}]},"a-types":{"type":"map","title":"Audience segment types","center":"Audiences","nodes":[{"t":"Your data","d":"Website visitors, app users, customer lists and YouTube viewers who have interacted with you directly — usually your highest-intent segments."},{"t":"Affinity","d":"Google-built segments based on long-term interests and habits, closer to a lifestyle than a purchase intent."},{"t":"In-market","d":"People Google believes are actively researching or comparing products like yours right now."},{"t":"Life events","d":"People around a major life change, such as moving house or graduating, when buying habits shift."},{"t":"Detailed demographics","d":"Groups defined by a shared life fact, such as parental status or homeownership, beyond basic age and gender."},{"t":"Custom segments","d":"An audience you build yourself from keywords, URLs or apps describing the kind of person you want to reach."}]},"a-retarget":{"type":"steps","title":"The remarketing ladder","steps":[{"t":"All visitors","d":"The broadest remarketing list: everyone who has been to the site recently. Low cost, low intent."},{"t":"Viewed a product/page","d":"Narrower, tied to a specific interest shown on-site."},{"t":"Added to cart, didn't buy","d":"High-intent group that dropped off near the finish line — usually the best remarketing ROI."},{"t":"Past purchasers","d":"For cross-sell, upsell or repeat-purchase campaigns, often excluded from acquisition campaigns instead."},{"t":"Customer Match upload","d":"Your own CRM or email list uploaded directly, for targeting or for exclusion from prospecting campaigns."}]},"c-obj":{"type":"tree","title":"Which campaign type should I use?","start":"goal","nodes":{"goal":{"q":"What's the main goal?","o":[["Get leads or sales from people already searching","search"],["Sell physical products online","shopping"],["Build awareness or get in front of new people","awareness"],["Not sure — I have a mix of goals","mix"]]},"search":{"q":"Do you have a usable landing page and at least some conversion tracking?","o":[["Yes, both are ready","search-ready"],["Not yet","search-notready"]]},"search-ready":{"a":["Build a Search campaign, splitting brand and non-brand into separate campaigns.","Many advertisers start with Manual CPC or Maximize clicks while conversion data is thin, then move to a conversion-based strategy such as Target CPA. Check the current Google Ads Help Centre for current guidance.","Write at least two ad groups, each with a tight keyword theme."],"title":"Go with Search"},"search-notready":{"a":["Fix tracking and the landing page first — Search campaigns waste budget fastest without them.","Set up conversion actions and verify the Google tag fires.","Come back to campaign creation once a conversion can be recorded end to end."],"title":"Fix the basics first"},"shopping":{"q":"Do you have a product feed live in Merchant Center?","o":[["Yes, feed is live","shopping-ready"],["No feed yet","shopping-notready"]]},"shopping-ready":{"a":["Launch a Shopping campaign or an ecommerce Performance Max campaign using that feed.","Start with Maximize conversion value, unconstrained by a target ROAS, until you have a meaningful number of conversions; check the current Google Ads Help Centre for current guidance.","Keep a parallel brand Search campaign running so brand terms aren't left only to PMax."],"title":"Go with Shopping / Performance Max"},"shopping-notready":{"a":["Set up Merchant Center and get your feed approved first — Shopping and ecommerce PMax can't run without it.","Use a standard Search or Display campaign in the meantime if you need to start now."],"title":"Build the feed first"},"awareness":{"a":["Consider YouTube or Demand Gen for visual, discovery-style reach.","Use these to build an audience and content library you can remarket to later with Search.","Keep expectations realistic: this stage usually reports on views and engagement, not direct conversions."],"title":"Go with YouTube or Demand Gen"},"mix":{"a":["Start with Search for the highest-intent part of the goal — it's the easiest to read and prove value from.","Add other campaign types one at a time as you have the budget and tracking to evaluate each on its own.","Avoid launching everything at once; you won't be able to tell which one is doing the work."],"title":"Start narrow, then expand"}}},"c-abo":{"type":"tabs","title":"Search vs Performance Max vs Shopping vs Demand Gen","tabs":[{"t":"Search","p":"Text ads on search results, built from keywords you choose (or Google infers via AI Max for Search). Best control, clearest reporting, usually the strongest ROI for people already looking to buy."},{"t":"Performance Max","p":"One goal-based campaign that runs across Search, Display, YouTube, Discover, Gmail and Maps from a single set of assets and signals, with the AI choosing where to spend. Less visibility per channel, but can find incremental conversions humans wouldn't have targeted manually."},{"t":"Shopping","p":"Product listings built from your Merchant Center feed, shown with image, price and store name. The clearest fit for ecommerce with a live product catalogue."},{"t":"Demand Gen","p":"Visual, discovery-style placements across YouTube, Discover and Gmail, aimed at creating interest rather than capturing an existing search. Works best alongside Search, not instead of it."}]},"c-check":{"type":"check","title":"Pre-launch checklist","items":["Conversion tracking tested end-to-end, at least one real conversion recorded","Currency and time zone confirmed correct","Privacy policy page live and linked","Campaign type matches the stated goal","Negative keywords added at the account and campaign level","Ad groups split by a tight keyword theme, not a broad dump","At least two ads per ad group, with Ad Strength used as a guide rather than a target","Sitelinks, callouts and structured snippets added","Daily budget set deliberately, not left at the default suggestion","Location option (Presence, or Presence or interest) chosen deliberately","Bid strategy chosen to match how much conversion data you already have","Auto-tagging turned on"]},"b-bid":{"type":"tabs","title":"Bid strategies, at a glance","tabs":[{"t":"Manual CPC","p":"You set each keyword's bid directly. Full control, no automation help — mostly a fallback or a baseline to compare automated strategies against."},{"t":"Maximize clicks","p":"Spends your budget to get as many clicks as possible. A volume strategy, not an efficiency one — useful for early traffic before you have conversion data."},{"t":"Target CPA","p":"Automated bidding aimed at a cost-per-conversion target you set. Needs real conversion history to work well. How it is offered, standalone or as a target within Maximize conversions, has changed over time, so check the current Google Ads Help Centre."},{"t":"Target ROAS","p":"Automated bidding aimed at a return-on-ad-spend target, using conversion value. The value-based counterpart to Target CPA, for accounts tracking revenue per conversion."},{"t":"Target impression share","p":"Bids to win a chosen share of auctions at a chosen position. A visibility strategy, not an efficiency one — common for brand campaigns."}]},"b-learn":{"type":"calc","title":"Learning-period simulator (a teaching simplification)","fields":[{"k":"conv","l":"Conversions per week before the edit","min":0,"max":50,"step":1,"v":5},{"k":"edit","l":"Size of the recent edit","o":[[1,"Small (minor budget tweak)"],[2,"Moderate (new target or audience change)"],[3,"Large (new bid strategy or campaign)"]],"v":2}],"run":function(v){const conv=Math.max(0,Math.min(50,+v.conv||0)),edit=Math.max(1,Math.min(3,+v.edit||1)),need=edit*15,days=conv>0?Math.ceil(need/(conv/7)):null,lbl=["","Small","Moderate","Large"][edit];
return `<div class="tiles"><div class="tile"><b>${days?days+" days":"—"}</b><span>rough time to exit the learning period</span></div><div class="tile"><b>${need}</b><span>conversions this model assumes are needed</span></div></div><p style="font-size:.875rem;color:var(--ink-soft);margin:8px 0 0">${lbl} edits need more fresh data before bidding stabilizes again. This is a simplified teaching model — Google doesn't publish an exact formula or timeline.</p>`}},"cr-test":{"type":"steps","title":"The creative test process","steps":[{"t":"Pick one variable","d":"A single headline, one image, one offer — not the whole ad."},{"t":"Keep everything else identical","d":"Same ad group, same targeting, same budget split, so the variable you changed is the only explanation for a difference."},{"t":"Run it long enough","d":"Let both variants gather a meaningful number of clicks and conversions before comparing — days, not hours."},{"t":"Compare against noise, not just the headline number","d":"Check whether the gap is bigger than what ordinary variation at that sample size would produce."},{"t":"Act on the winner, then test the next variable","d":"Keep the winning version as the new baseline and repeat with a different single change."}]},"cr-types":{"type":"map","title":"Creative types, at a glance","center":"Creative","nodes":[{"t":"RSA","d":"Responsive Search Ads: headlines and descriptions, for intent-driven Search campaigns."},{"t":"PMax assets","d":"Images, logos, video, headlines and descriptions organized into asset groups for cross-channel automated delivery."},{"t":"Demand Gen","d":"Visual, native-feeling creative for YouTube, Discover and Gmail placements, aimed at discovery rather than search intent."},{"t":"Display","d":"Image and HTML5 ads for the Display Network specifically, sized to standard ad placements."}]},"cr-split":{"type":"calc","title":"A/B result significance helper (a plain-English check, not a formal test)","fields":[{"k":"ca","l":"Variant A — clicks","min":0,"max":20000,"step":10,"v":1000},{"k":"xa","l":"Variant A — conversions","min":0,"max":2000,"step":1,"v":40},{"k":"cb","l":"Variant B — clicks","min":0,"max":20000,"step":10,"v":1000},{"k":"xb","l":"Variant B — conversions","min":0,"max":2000,"step":1,"v":55}],"run":function(v){const ca=Math.max(0,+v.ca||0),xa=Math.max(0,Math.min(ca,+v.xa||0)),cb=Math.max(0,+v.cb||0),xb=Math.max(0,Math.min(cb,+v.xb||0)),ra=ca>0?xa/ca:null,rb=cb>0?xb/cb:null;
if(ra===null||rb===null)return `<p>—</p><p style="font-size:.875rem;color:var(--ink-soft)">Enter clicks greater than zero for both variants.</p>`;
const diff=Math.abs(ra-rb),minSample=Math.min(ca,cb),rough=minSample<200?"Too little traffic yet to say much — keep running it.":minSample<1000?(diff>0.02?"A plausible real difference, but still check again at higher volume.":"Could easily be ordinary noise at this sample size."):(diff>0.01?"Large enough sample that this gap is likely a real difference.":"Close enough that it may not be worth acting on yet.");
return `<div class="tiles"><div class="tile"><b>${(ra*100).toFixed(1)}%</b><span>Variant A conversion rate</span></div><div class="tile"><b>${(rb*100).toFixed(1)}%</b><span>Variant B conversion rate</span></div></div><p style="margin-top:8px">${rough}</p><p style="font-size:.875rem;color:var(--ink-soft);margin:8px 0 0">This is a plain-English sanity check, not a formal statistical significance test.</p>`}},"t-calc":{"type":"calc","title":"Metric chain calculator","fields":[{"k":"imp","l":"Impressions","min":0,"max":1000000,"step":100,"v":50000},{"k":"ctr","l":"CTR (%)","min":0,"max":30,"step":0.1,"v":4},{"k":"cpc","l":"Avg. CPC ($)","min":0,"max":50,"step":0.1,"v":2},{"k":"cvr","l":"Conversion rate (%)","min":0,"max":50,"step":0.1,"v":5},{"k":"aov","l":"Avg. order value ($)","min":0,"max":2000,"step":1,"v":80}],"run":function(v){const imp=Math.max(0,+v.imp||0),ctr=Math.max(0,Math.min(100,+v.ctr||0))/100,cpc=Math.max(0,+v.cpc||0),cvr=Math.max(0,Math.min(100,+v.cvr||0))/100,aov=Math.max(0,+v.aov||0);
const clicks=imp*ctr,cost=clicks*cpc,conv=clicks*cvr,cpa=conv>0?cost/conv:null,value=conv*aov,roas=cost>0?value/cost:null;
return `<div class="tiles"><div class="tile"><b>${Math.round(clicks).toLocaleString()}</b><span>Clicks</span></div><div class="tile"><b>$${cost.toFixed(2)}</b><span>Cost</span></div><div class="tile"><b>${(Math.round(conv*10)/10).toLocaleString()}</b><span>Conversions</span></div><div class="tile"><b>${cpa!==null?"$"+cpa.toFixed(2):"—"}</b><span>CPA</span></div><div class="tile"><b>${roas!==null?roas.toFixed(2)+"x":"—"}</b><span>ROAS</span></div></div>`}},"t-diag":{"type":"tree","title":"What does this signal mean?","start":"pick","nodes":{"pick":{"q":"What are you seeing in the account?","o":[["Impression share lost to budget is high","lost-budget"],["Impression share lost to rank is high","lost-rank"],["CTR dropped suddenly","ctr-drop"],["Conversions dropped but clicks look normal","conv-drop"]]},"lost-budget":{"a":["Your budget is the limiting factor, not your bids or quality.","Consider a higher budget on this campaign, or reallocate from a lower-performing one.","Check impression share by hour to see if it's capped early in the day."],"title":"Budget is the constraint"},"lost-rank":{"a":["Your Ad Rank isn't high enough to show more often, even with budget available.","Check bid, expected CTR, ad relevance and landing page experience individually — Ad Rank is built from all four.","A bid increase alone may not help if quality signals are weak."],"title":"Ad Rank is the constraint"},"ctr-drop":{"a":["Check whether a competitor's new ad, a seasonal shift, or your own recent ad edit lines up with the timing.","Review Ad Strength and whether pinning reduced the combinations being tested.","Confirm the ad is still showing in the position it used to — a lower position alone lowers CTR."],"title":"Investigate CTR"},"conv-drop":{"a":["This usually points to tracking, not performance — check the troubleshooting module's order (tracking first).","Confirm the Google tag is still firing and the gclid isn't being dropped.","Check whether a Primary conversion action setting changed."],"title":"Check tracking first"}}},"t-attr":{"type":"tabs","title":"Attribution models and windows","tabs":[{"t":"Data-driven attribution","p":"Google Ads' default model. Distributes credit for a conversion across the different ads and clicks that contributed to it, based on patterns in your own account's data, rather than crediting only the last click."},{"t":"Conversion windows","p":"How long after a click or a view a conversion can still be counted. A longer window captures slower-deciding customers but delays how complete your reporting looks in the days right after a click."},{"t":"View-through conversions","p":"People who saw but didn't click an ad, mostly on Display and video, and converted later. Directionally useful, but a weaker, noisier signal of causation than a click-based conversion."}]},"tr-tree":{"type":"tree","title":"Troubleshooting decision tree","start":"pick","nodes":{"pick":{"q":"What best describes the problem?","o":[["Conversions dropped or look wrong","c1"],["Ads aren't showing, or spend is far too low","d1"],["Spend is steady but cost per conversion is climbing","k1"],["Plenty of clicks but nobody converts","q1"]]},"c1":{"q":"Does any conversion action show a status like 'No recent conversions' or 'Unverified'?","o":[["Yes","c-tag"],["No, the statuses look fine","c2"]]},"c2":{"q":"Did the website, checkout or cookie banner change in the last few weeks?","o":[["Yes","c-site"],["No, nothing changed","c-lag"]]},"c-tag":{"title":"The tag is probably not firing","a":["Open Tag Assistant or Tag Manager preview and load the conversion page to confirm the Google tag fires.","Check that the conversion action is still the one on the page, and that it hasn't been duplicated or removed.","Fix the tag first. Don't touch bids until conversions record again."]},"c-site":{"title":"A site or consent change is the likely cause","a":["Ask what changed: theme, checkout, form plugin, redirects or the cookie banner.","Test a real conversion end to end and confirm the gclid survives the redirects.","Check Consent Mode and Enhanced conversions are still configured after the change."]},"c-lag":{"title":"Check lag, double counting and the comparison","a":["Recent days always look low because of conversion lag. Compare periods that are old enough to be complete.","Look for duplicate tags that would inflate a number, or a changed Primary conversion setting.","Compare with your CRM or store data and decide whether the gap is stable and explainable."]},"d1":{"q":"Does any campaign, ad or billing notice show Disapproved, Limited, Under review or a payment problem?","o":[["Yes","d-policy"],["No, everything is approved","d2"]]},"d2":{"q":"What do the impression share columns show?","o":[["Lost to budget is high","d-budget"],["Lost to rank is high","d-rank"],["Neither; the share is fine but volume is small","d-demand"]]},"d-policy":{"title":"Fix status and policy first","a":["Open the Policy manager and read the exact reason for each flag.","Fix the ad or the landing page, then resubmit. Appeal only if you believe the decision was wrong.","Resolve any billing alert. Never open a new account to get around a suspension."]},"d-budget":{"title":"Budget is the constraint","a":["Raise the budget in a modest step, or move spend from a weaker campaign.","Check impression share by hour to see if the budget runs out early in the day.","Judge the result after a full cycle before the next step."]},"d-rank":{"title":"Ad Rank is the constraint","a":["Check expected CTR, ad relevance and landing page experience one by one.","A higher bid alone may not help if quality signals are weak.","Review assets and ad copy for specificity, and confirm the keywords match the ad group theme."]},"d-demand":{"title":"Demand or targeting is limiting volume","a":["Check whether location, audience or schedule settings are narrower than intended.","Add related keyword themes or a new ad group, one change at a time.","Accept that some niches are small, and consider a second channel."]},"k1":{"q":"Did average CPC rise, or did conversion rate fall?","o":[["CPC rose","k-cpc"],["Conversion rate fell","k-cvr"],["Both moved a little","k-both"]]},"k-cpc":{"title":"Look at the auction and match drift","a":["Open Auction Insights for new or more aggressive competitors.","Review the search terms report for drift into expensive or irrelevant queries.","Check quality signals and recent bid strategy or target changes in the change history."]},"k-cvr":{"title":"Look at the page, the offer and tracking","a":["Review the landing page for speed, message match and form friction, on mobile first.","Segment by device, location and hour to see where conversion rate fell.","Rule out tracking changes before blaming the page."]},"k-both":{"title":"Compare equal periods and check the change log","a":["Compare the same weekdays and segment by device, network and location.","Read the change history for edits near the date the numbers moved.","Consider seasonality or a competitor promotion before changing anything."]},"q1":{"q":"Do the search terms match what you actually sell?","o":[["Yes, they look right","q-page"],["No, many are off-topic","q-terms"]]},"q-page":{"title":"The landing page is the next suspect","a":["Check mobile speed, headline match, a clear next step and a short form.","Confirm that prices or offers in the ad are true on the page.","Test one change at a time and judge it against a sensible sample size."]},"q-terms":{"title":"Tighten the traffic","a":["Add the off-topic searches as negative keywords at the right level.","Review match types and the Broad match keywords that are drifting.","Check that the bid strategy is learning from real, correct conversions."]}}},"sc-calc":{"type":"calc","title":"Budget ramp planner","fields":[{"k":"cur","l":"Current daily budget ($)","min":5,"max":5000,"step":5,"v":50},{"k":"tgt","l":"Target daily budget ($)","min":5,"max":20000,"step":5,"v":150},{"k":"pct","l":"Increase per step (%)","min":5,"max":30,"step":1,"v":15},{"k":"gap","l":"Days between steps","min":3,"max":21,"step":1,"v":7}],"run":function(v){const cur=Math.max(5,Math.min(5000,+v.cur||5)),tgt=Math.max(5,Math.min(20000,+v.tgt||5)),pct=Math.max(5,Math.min(30,+v.pct||5)),gap=Math.max(3,Math.min(21,Math.round(+v.gap||3)));
if(tgt<=cur)return `<p><b>The target must be higher than the current budget.</b></p><p style="font-size:.875rem;color:var(--ink-soft);margin:8px 0 0">Raise the target or lower the current budget to see a ramp.</p>`;
const r=1+pct/100,steps=Math.max(1,Math.ceil(Math.log(tgt/cur)/Math.log(r)-1e-9)),days=steps*gap;let b=cur;const rows=[];for(let i=0;i<steps;i++){b=Math.min(tgt,b*r);rows.push(b)}
const shown=rows.slice(0,10),more=rows.length>10?`<p style="font-size:.875rem;color:var(--ink-soft);margin:4px 0 0">Showing the first 10 of ${rows.length} steps.</p>`:"";
const note=pct>20?"Steps above about 20% are more likely to restart learning, so expect more volatility.":"This step size is in the range this course recommends.";
return `<div class="tiles"><div class="tile"><b>${steps}</b><span>steps to reach the target</span></div><div class="tile"><b>${days} days</b><span>about ${(days/7).toFixed(1)} weeks at ${gap} days per step</span></div><div class="tile"><b>$${Math.round(tgt*30.4).toLocaleString()}</b><span>approx. monthly cap at the target (30.4 × daily)</span></div></div>`+
shown.map((x,i)=>`<div class="bar ${i===shown.length-1&&rows.length<=10?'w':''}"><span>Step ${i+1}</span><i style="width:${Math.max(4,x/tgt*60)}%"></i><span>$${Math.round(x).toLocaleString()}</span></div>`).join("")+more+`<p style="font-size:.875rem;color:var(--ink-soft);margin:8px 0 0">${note} Judge CPA or ROAS after each step before taking the next. This is a planning model, not a forecast of results.</p>`}},"sc-cadence":{"type":"tabs","title":"Optimization cadence","tabs":[{"t":"Daily","p":"About five minutes. Scan for disapprovals, billing alerts, sudden spend swings and conversion status. Do not change bids or targets on one day of data."},{"t":"Weekly","p":"Thirty to sixty minutes. Review search terms and add negatives, look at asset and ad performance for clear losers, check budgets and impression share lost to budget, check monthly pacing, and write down every change you make."},{"t":"Monthly","p":"Half a day. Compare results with targets, segment by device, location and hour, review landing pages, read the results of any experiments, report to the client or stakeholder, and plan next month's tests."},{"t":"Quarterly","p":"A strategy review. Recheck unit economics and targets, campaign structure, new campaign types or markets, and competitors. Retire what isn't working and set the goals for the next quarter."}]},"sc-pace":{"type":"calc","title":"Monthly pacing check","fields":[{"k":"mb","l":"Monthly budget ($)","min":100,"max":100000,"step":100,"v":3000},{"k":"dm","l":"Days in the month","min":28,"max":31,"step":1,"v":31},{"k":"d","l":"Days elapsed","min":1,"max":31,"step":1,"v":10},{"k":"sp","l":"Spend so far ($)","min":0,"max":100000,"step":10,"v":1200}],"run":function(v){const mb=Math.max(100,+v.mb||100),dm=Math.max(28,Math.min(31,Math.round(+v.dm||31))),d=Math.max(1,Math.min(dm,Math.round(+v.d||1))),sp=Math.max(0,+v.sp||0);
const exp=mb*d/dm,proj=sp/d*dm,diff=(proj/mb-1)*100,left=dm-d,need=left>0?Math.max(0,mb-sp)/left:null;
const status=diff>10?"Pacing high":diff<-10?"Pacing low":"On track",tone=diff>10?"A review is worth it: check whether the extra spend is buying conversions at a good CPA.":diff<-10?"Find the reason before raising the budget: lost to rank, narrow targeting or pending approvals.":"Within about 10% of plan. Keep watching weekly.";
return `<div class="tiles"><div class="tile"><b>${status}</b><span>${diff>=0?"+":""}${diff.toFixed(0)}% vs monthly budget at this rate</span></div><div class="tile"><b>$${Math.round(proj).toLocaleString()}</b><span>projected month-end spend</span></div><div class="tile"><b>$${Math.round(exp).toLocaleString()}</b><span>expected spend by now</span></div><div class="tile"><b>${need===null?"—":"$"+need.toFixed(2)}</b><span>${need===null?"no days left in the month":"average daily spend needed to land on budget"}</span></div></div><p style="margin-top:8px">${tone}</p><p style="font-size:.875rem;color:var(--ink-soft);margin:8px 0 0">A daily budget is an average, and billing is capped at roughly 30.4 times the average daily budget over a month. This tool projects a straight line, so it ignores weekday patterns and seasonality.</p>`}},"ag-own":{"type":"map","title":"Who owns what in a client engagement","center":"Client assets","nodes":[{"t":"Ads account","d":"Client. It holds the campaign history that bidding has learned from. You get access through your manager account and lose none of it if you leave."},{"t":"Billing","d":"Client. Ad spend is their money and liability, so their payment method belongs on the billing profile. Your fee is a separate invoice."},{"t":"Analytics","d":"Client. The GA4 property is long-term measurement for the business. Ask for an editor or admin role for yourself instead of creating it under your login."},{"t":"Tag Manager","d":"Client owns the container; the agency publishes changes as a named user, so the site never depends on an outside login."},{"t":"Merchant","d":"Client. The product feed is tied to the store and its policy standing. Suspensions follow the account, so it must be theirs."},{"t":"Domain","d":"Client. The website and domain are core business assets and must never sit under an agency login."}]},"ag-onb":{"type":"steps","title":"The client onboarding sequence","steps":[{"t":"Discovery","d":"Understand the business, the offer, margins, the sales cycle and what success means. Capture it in a short written brief."},{"t":"Access","d":"Request a manager link or user invitation for each account, at the lowest access level that works. Record who holds what."},{"t":"Audit","d":"Check conversion tracking, linked accounts, account structure, search terms, policy status and the landing pages before changing anything."},{"t":"Baselines","d":"Record current spend, conversions, CPA or ROAS, and impression share. Agree the KPIs and targets in writing."},{"t":"30-day plan","d":"List the first fixes and tests in priority order, with owners and dates, and what you need from the client."},{"t":"Reporting","d":"Agree the report format, the cadence of updates and calls, response times and who approves spend changes."}]},"ag-hand":{"type":"check","title":"Handover checklist","items":["Confirm the client has admin access on every account","Confirm the billing profile is in the client's name","Confirm GA4, Tag Manager and Merchant Center are owned by the client","Export final performance data and a closing report","Document the structure, targets and key settings","Share the change log and the test log","Hand over creative files and asset libraries as agreed in the contract","Agree a date to remove your manager link and user access","Remove your access once the client confirms","Record the handover in your own files and ask for feedback"]},"p-case":{"type":"tabs","title":"Case study template (illustrative example)","tabs":[{"t":"Situation","p":"State the business, the goal and the starting point. Example (illustrative): a local garden-maintenance firm with a working website, no conversion tracking and a budget spread across one broad campaign."},{"t":"Actions","p":"Say what you did and why, in order. Example (illustrative): fixed form and call tracking, split brand and non-brand, grouped keywords by service, added negatives from the search terms report, and rewrote ads around specific services."},{"t":"Results","p":"Give numbers with a time frame and a baseline, and be honest about what you can't prove. Example (illustrative): cost per lead moved from 40 to 28 over eight weeks on similar spend. Mention seasonality and anything else that changed."},{"t":"Lessons","p":"Say what you would do differently and what you learned. This shows judgement, which clients value more than a perfect story. Get permission before naming a client or using real figures."}]},"p-radar":{"type":"calc","title":"Skills radar (0 = new to it, 5 = confident)","fields":[{"k":"s0","l":"Campaign building","min":0,"max":5,"step":1,"v":3},{"k":"s1","l":"Tracking and analytics","min":0,"max":5,"step":1,"v":2},{"k":"s2","l":"Creative and copy","min":0,"max":5,"step":1,"v":3},{"k":"s3","l":"Strategy and scaling","min":0,"max":5,"step":1,"v":2},{"k":"s4","l":"Client communication","min":0,"max":5,"step":1,"v":3},{"k":"s5","l":"Commercial and selling","min":0,"max":5,"step":1,"v":1}],"run":function(v){const L=["Campaign build","Tracking","Creative","Strategy","Clients","Commercial"],M=["Module 4","Modules 2 and 7","Module 6","Module 9","Module 10","Module 11"],n=6,cx=260,cy=165,R=105;
const s=L.map((_,i)=>{const x=Math.round(+v["s"+i]);return Number.isFinite(x)?Math.max(0,Math.min(5,x)):0});
const pt=(i,k)=>{const a=-Math.PI/2+i*2*Math.PI/n;return[cx+R*k/5*Math.cos(a),cy+R*k/5*Math.sin(a)]};
const ring=k=>`<polygon points="${L.map((_,i)=>pt(i,k).map(c=>c.toFixed(1)).join(",")).join(" ")}" fill="none" stroke-width="1" style="stroke:var(--line)"/>`;
let g=[1,2,3,4,5].map(ring).join("");
g+=L.map((_,i)=>{const p=pt(i,5);return `<line x1="${cx}" y1="${cy}" x2="${p[0].toFixed(1)}" y2="${p[1].toFixed(1)}" stroke-width="1" style="stroke:var(--line)"/>`}).join("");
g+=`<polygon points="${s.map((k,i)=>pt(i,k).map(c=>c.toFixed(1)).join(",")).join(" ")}" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linejoin="round" style="fill:var(--g-blue-600);fill-opacity:.22;stroke:var(--g-blue-600)"/>`;
g+=s.map((k,i)=>{const p=pt(i,k);return `<circle cx="${p[0].toFixed(1)}" cy="${p[1].toFixed(1)}" r="5" style="fill:var(--g-${COLS[i%4]});stroke:var(--bg);stroke-width:1.5"/>`}).join("");
g+=L.map((t,i)=>{const a=-Math.PI/2+i*2*Math.PI/n,c=Math.cos(a),x=cx+(R+18)*c,y=cy+(R+18)*Math.sin(a)+4,an=Math.abs(c)<.2?"middle":c>0?"start":"end";return `<text x="${x.toFixed(1)}" y="${y.toFixed(1)}" font-size="13" text-anchor="${an}" style="fill:var(--ink);font-family:var(--hd)">${t} ${s[i]}</text>`}).join("");
const avg=s.reduce((a,b)=>a+b,0)/n,lo=s.indexOf(Math.min(...s)),hi=s.indexOf(Math.max(...s));
return `<svg viewBox="0 0 520 330" role="img" aria-label="Skills radar chart"><title>Skills radar chart</title><desc>Radar chart of six self-rated skill areas, each from 0 to 5.</desc>${g}</svg><div class="tiles"><div class="tile"><b>${avg.toFixed(1)} / 5</b><span>average across six skills</span></div><div class="tile"><b>${L[hi]}</b><span>strongest area (${s[hi]})</span></div><div class="tile"><b>${L[lo]}</b><span>next area to study (${s[lo]}), see ${M[lo]}</span></div></div><p style="font-size:.875rem;color:var(--ink-soft);margin:8px 0 0">A self-assessment, not a test. Revisit it after each module.</p>`}},"p-path":{"type":"tree","title":"Which path suits you first?","start":"start","nodes":{"start":{"q":"What do you want your working week to look like?","o":[["Independence, with a few clients I know well","f1"],["Variety, and a team to learn from","a1"],["Depth in one business and its growth","i1"]]},"f1":{"q":"Are you comfortable finding clients, pricing your work and chasing invoices yourself?","o":[["Yes, or willing to learn","p-free"],["Not really","p-agency-first"]]},"a1":{"q":"Do you prefer breadth across many industries over depth in one?","o":[["Yes, breadth","p-agency"],["No, I want depth","i1"]]},"i1":{"q":"Are your analytics and cross-team communication skills strong, or are you building them?","o":[["Strong or building steadily","p-inhouse"],["Both need a lot of work","p-agency-first"]]},"p-free":{"title":"Freelance looks like a fit","a":["Build two or three documented projects and a simple case study first.","Offer a defined audit package to people who already trust you.","Set up contracts, ownership rules and invoicing before the first client."]},"p-agency":{"title":"Agency looks like a fit","a":["Apply for junior paid-media roles and show a project with real numbers.","Learn the processes: onboarding, reporting, QA and change logs.","Use the exposure to many accounts to find the niche you like most."]},"p-inhouse":{"title":"In-house looks like a fit","a":["Learn the business side: margins, lifetime value and the sales funnel.","Strengthen analytics, GA4 and CRM reconciliation skills.","Target roles at companies with meaningful budgets and a clear product."]},"p-agency-first":{"title":"Build skills in a team before going alone","a":["An agency or a junior role gives structure, mentoring and real accounts.","Use it to build selling, reporting and communication skills.","Revisit freelance or in-house options after a year or so of proof."]}}}};
